\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Share Traders Lose Hope for Quick  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2016\par 05:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A Nikkei recovery is losing steam as Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda says there are no specific comments to report from his meeting with Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . Earlier, the news of the meeting raised speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would unload an emergency policy move. But Kuroda says the meeting was just a regular catch-up on monetary policy matters. The Nikkei is down 3.9% at 15061.53 versus down 2.9% earlier. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] JF Apex Securities says Malaysia's Oriental Holdings (4006.KU) and \uc2 Kulim Malaysia\uc2  (2003.KU) are stocks to watch today. Oriental Holdings is acquiring a 90% stake in PT Sumatera Sawit Lestari, an Indonesian company that owns 8,000 hectares of oil palm land, in a move to expand its oil palm plantation landbank there. At noon the share price of Oriental is flat at 6.58 ringgit. Kulim is acquiring four Indonesia based plantation companies for approximately MYR509.4 million ($123 million), thus increasing its total cultivated plantation land bank from its present 47,010 hectares to 81,392 hectares. In a filing to \uc2 Bursa Malaysia\uc2 , Kulim said the acquisition is in line with the group's business direction to expand its plantation land bank and gain larger market access in Indonesia. Share price of Kulim is up 0.53% at MYR 3.78 ringgit with 326,700 shares traded.(celine.fernandez@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0414 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Japanese government should scrap plans to raise the sales tax again next year, says Etsuro Honda, a close adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Japanese consumers are still struggling with a similar tax increase implemented in April 2014 that derailed a recovery. "It should be made [a] priority to stimulate demand through both fiscal and monetary policies, and if necessary the government should put together a supplementary budget in the autumn or later," Honda says. (henry.hoenig@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0412 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  could hold an emergency meeting if it believes it is at risk of losing the fight to eradicate Japan's "deflationary mindset," a close adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday. With stocks tumbling and the yen rising, already sagging inflation expectations may weaken further, says Etsuro Honda, a former finance ministry bureaucrat. If the BOJ does act before its next scheduled meeting in mid-March, it would need to expand its asset-purchase program and push a key short-term rate further below zero, he says. (henry.hoenig@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0410 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese lawmaker Kozo Yamamoto says the market turmoil in Japan has global origins and a global response is required. In an interview, Yamamoto says it's a problem that global policies "aren't harmonized," observing that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  recently raised interest rates while central banks in Europe and Japan have been lowering them. He suggests that Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  call an emergency summit. Japan is already set to play host to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of Seven\f28 \uc2 \f2  summit of industrialized nations in late May. One potential pitfall for global coordination: While the yen's sharp rise is likely to hit Japanese corporate profits, the U.S. and some other trading partners might actually welcome the fall in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the yen. (peter.landers@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei recoups some losses following the news that Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda and the finance ministry's Masatsugu Asakawa, Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bureaucrat, entered the Prime Minister's office building. The meeting among top financial policy makers is raising speculation for any potential policy action to stem the yen's latest rise. The Nikkei is now down 2.9% at 15258.56. At one point earlier in the day, the index was down 5.4%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The mood has shifted in Tokyo after the yen's sudden rise and the Nikkei Stock Average's continued fall. Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda scurries to the prime minister's residence after a parliamentary appearance, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's top official on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  matters, Masatsugu Asakawa, joins them. It's an unusual and unscheduled meeting of the top decision-makers. Officials are under pressure to do something about the rapid market shifts, and the Nikkei recovers a bit in the early afternoon. But so far the official line is simply to express concern and avoid specific comments about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or other potential action. (peter.landers@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0322 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The correlation between crude oil price and Malaysia's ringgit appears to be weakening, at least temporarily. While Brent, the international crude oil benchmark, is up 4.2% from its Thursday close, the ringgit has slipped 0.7% against the U.S. dollar. Up till a week ago, the ringgit would have rallied if crude oil prices rose. But the recent overturning of expectations as to when U.S. interest rates might be raised again seems to have superseded the oil-ringgit linkage. Another explanation for the ringgit's lack of reaction to crude's rally is that the re-runs of rumors that oil producers might agree to coordinated production-cuts is wearing thin on ringgit traders. Oil prices have experienced extreme swings on speculation of oil supply cuts, followed by denials from officials of oil-producing countries. USD/MYR is now 4.1650 from Thursday's close of 4.1350. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] It may seem premature to consider the likelihood of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  easing policy again at its April meeting, but the current level of USD/SGD could present a timely opportunity to place bearish bets on the Singapore dollar. USD/SGD is climbing back above the long-term chart resistance of the 200-day moving average, nullifying yesterday's downwards breach. If USD/SGD ends Friday above 1.3920, the 200-DMA will be reinstated as a chart support, which might spur short-covering of U.S. dollars. The possibility that MAS will weaken the Singapore dollar, via a shifting of the midpoint of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trading band, has been raised by analysts after 14 months of negative inflation, faltering economic growth and the dovish moves of other global central banks. The MAS last eased policy in October, reducing the slope of the managed-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation path. USD/SGD is now at 1.3952 from its Thursday close of 1.3894. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0251 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] OCBC says it remains cautious about increasing expenses at \f28 \uc2 \f2 SMRT Corp. (S53.SG) \f28 \uc2 \f2 and higher competition after shares outperform the local benchmark. OCBC notes that SMRT's shares have risen 6.3% so far this year, in contrast to a 12% decline in the FTSE Straits Times Index. The house says the outperformance can likely be explained by the better-than-expected third quarter earnings. However, revenues may be weaker ahead as a new train line in Singapore takes away some passengers and a fare reduction cut takes effect. OCBC reiterates its hold view on SMRT but raises its fair value estimate to S$1.51 from S$1.43 to account for the stronger third quarter earnings. SMRT is up 0.3% at S$1.605, compared with a 0.2% gain by the Straits Times Index. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0246 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold prices eased in Asia trade from a one-year high touched the previous day as dollar weakness and tumbling equity markets fueled a recent rally. Gold briefly pushed above $1,260/oz level due to rising safe-haven demand that has made it the standout commodity this year. The rally in gold is seen intact with the U.S. Federal Reserve Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  signaling that interest rates won't be increased if market turmoil persists. Spot gold is trading at $1,235.92/oz, down from the previous close at $1,246.57/oz. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0245 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesia announced the opening of 35 sectors to full foreign ownership Thursday, but "the jury is still out if the changes... will bring in fresh foreign investment into the economy," DBS says in a research note Friday. "The authorities suggest these changes as 'revolutionary," and "the broad scope of the revision should ease doubts about the government's attitude toward foreign investment," the bank says. "Yet, the [change] falls short on several fronts. Infrastructure-related sectors are barely [touched]." There was no mention of airport and seaport operators and, in the face of logistics inefficiencies, the government of Southeast Asia's largest economy touched only two public works subsectors, DBS says, adding that foreign investors had already been allowed up to 95% stake in some of the sectors fully opened, like toll-road \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and non-toxic waste management. "The change to 100% is far from being revolutionary. One wonders if foreign investors would be interested in operating toll-roads anyway, especially away from the main island of Java."(ben.otto@wsj.com; @benottoWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 11, 2016 23:31 ET (04:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160212ec2c000a6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
