\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Shares Tumble Following U.S. Drop and Oil Decline -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2016\par 01:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares in Asia plunged early Friday, as sharp declines overseas and a further tumble in oil to fresh multi-year lows fueled investor anxiety. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was down 3.7% at 15123.98, even as the Japanese yen eased off its strongest level against the dollar since November 2014. Japan's market was closed for national holiday Thursday, where markets in the region were mostly down, including the Hang Seng which fell nearly 4%. A stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has battered the Nikkei, which is headed to lose 10.6% this week. On Friday, the Japanese yen was last up 0.4% at Y111.93 to one U.S. dollar from the previous day's close. Japanese government bonds yielded 0.006% compared to 0.016% the previous day. Yields move inversely to prices. Elsewhere in shares, Australia's S&P \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 fell 0.6% early Friday, putting the benchmark on track to lose 4% this week. Markets in China are closed this week for the Lunar New Year holiday.(chao.deng@wsj.com; @chao_deng) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0033 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD is hovering near its Friday opening level, caught between opposing forces of persistent weakness in USD/JPY, and rising risk aversion in global stocks which is lifting the haven greenback elsewhere in Asia. USD/SGD is sensitive to external risk events, but the Singapore dollar's value is tied to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which include the Japanese yen. As the yen has been strengthening due to risk aversion, it makes it harder to expect the Singapore dollar to depreciate. The possibility of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  deciding to further ease policy, in light of various other central banks walking the dovish path, might however be the key to a weakening of the Singapore dollar. Though the MAS isn't scheduled to review monetary policy until April, bets on a more dovish stance could perpetuate much earlier than that. USD/SGD is now 1.3895 from its Thursday close of 1.3894. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0032 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW is rising Friday, implying a weaker South Korea won versus the U.S. dollar, as risk aversion hits its full stride. The overnight tumble of U.S. stocks, accompanied by a surge in gold to its one-year high, has further instigated a culling of risky assets. The won's slide is augmented by a 0.9% decline in the domestic stock market index, which is being dragged lower not only by Wall Street's 1.2% overnight loss, but also Japan's Nikkei slumping 4.4% thus far. USD/KRW will take on a bullish technical bias, suggesting further losses for the won ahead, if it closes Friday above 1,213 and inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. USD/KRW is now 1,204.5 from its Thursday close of 1,202.5. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0032 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Watch out for inventories when Japan releases fourth quarter GDP. Inventories likely shaved 0.1 percentage point off of growth, according to a survey of 20 economists by The Wall Street Journal. That means that companies are finally getting rid of inventories that have piled up in recent months, technically a good sign, some economists say, even though it could push the headline GDP figure negative. "A slowing in the pace of inventories piling up is likely to [have] helped pull down the overall growth rate," say SMBC Nikko Securities economists. They said companies likely drew down inventories of manufactured goods and slowed stockpiling raw materials. Japan's economy likely shrank an annualized 1.2% in the fourth quarter of 2015, according to a survey of 20 economists by the Wall Street Journal. The GDP data is due Sunday at 2350 GMT. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0032 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are defying gravity, holding up near their opening levels, despite an overnight tanking of USD/JPY which dented the benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  U.S. dollar index again. The magnetism of USD/JPY on USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs appears to be diminishing, and that could be a bad sign for risk-appetite. If USD/Asia pairs continue to hold their ground, it likely means that there is strong buying propping them up--which suggests risk aversion has returned to the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  arena. In the past two weeks, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have gained significantly against the U.S. dollar, due to a expectations that U.S. interest rates won't be raised again so soon. The trimming of prior long-USD positions is likely done, therefore USD/Asia pairs might start to rebound due to bearish bets against risky assets. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0031 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japan's economy likely shrank an annualized 1.2% in the fourth quarter of 2015, according to a survey of 20 economists by The Wall Street Journal, the second quarter of contraction out of the last three. "In addition to the negative headline figure, the contents are also likely to be bad, showing Japan's economy in a very tough spot," says Yoshiki Shinke of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dai-ichi Life Research Institute\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The decline is likely to come on the back of a slowdown in spending by households and businesses. One likely bright spot in the GDP data is an increase in exports is expected. Economists surveyed forecast exports up 0.5% on quarter, the second quarter of increase, and that overseas demand contributed 0.1 percentage points to growth. The GDP data is due Sunday at 2350 GMT. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18:43 ET - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (RIO.AU) is weaker early in Sydney--although not by as much as some might have expected. There's been a mixed reaction to the miner's announcement, after the Australian market closed on Thursday, that it will scrap its progressive dividend policy. "Overall, we regard the change in dividend policy as an appropriate response to the downturn," says Ord Minnett. "However, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  no longer offers an attractive dividend yield beyond CY16 based on our forecast prices, which could see some income-focused investors exit the stock over the near term," it says. Rio's London stock closed down 3.3%. Its Australian shares are trading down 0.7% at A$40.71. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18:41 ET - It's fairly standard in standstill agreements when big investors who have been making waves take board seats, and here is how it is worded in \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2 's regulatory filing about its agreement to give board representation to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carl Icahn\f28 \uc2 \f2 's firm and billionaire \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Paulson\f28 \uc2 \f2  of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paulson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .: The parties agree not to "make, or cause to be made, by press release or other public statement to the press or media, any statement or announcement that constitutes an ad hominem attack on, or otherwise disparages" the company, its officers or its directors or those who have served as such in the past. In general, pact runs through Aug. 1. (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2338 GMT - Barclays chief Japan economist Kyohei Morita announces his new "baseline scenario" tipping the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to take additional easing at a planned March 14-15 policy meeting. The BOJ will likely deepen the negative deposit rate to minus 0.3% from minus 0.1%, he says. Or, if it turns out that Japan's banking sector still needs more time to adjust to the negative-rate environment, the BOJ may instead expand its annual asset purchase program to a range of Y90 trillion to Y100 trillion, from the current Y80 trillion, he says. Morita's new forecast comes as the resurgent yen fuels a perception that the BOJ just has no choice but to move to arrest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gain, or its campaign to defeat deflation could fall apart. (Takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2332 GMT -- Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities likely continue to jawbone JPY's rapid strength recently, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely, says a senior Japanese bank dealer. "The pace (of the yen's strength) is unexpectedly fast," says the dealer. Speculators have found it easier to go ahead to buy JPY aggressively to test the USD/JPY's downside yesterday (hit as low as 110.98 from Y121.10 just 10 days ago) as they thought \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was unlikely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on national holiday. Today, Tokyo may send messages in the form of jawboning or the BOJ's rate check with banks given the rapid pace of the JPY strength. But the dealer cast doubts about probability of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the current level (the USD/JPY still higher than 78 when Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2011.)(hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2318 GMT - RBA Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glenn Stevens\f28 \uc2 \f2  tells parliament the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  retains flexibility to cut interest rates if the economy needs a jolt. On assessing recent global market volatility, Mr. Stevens it will be "a little while yet" before the impact of that can be properly understood. He also forecast moderate economic growth and indicated surprise at the recent pace of employment growth. The Australian dollar is higher after the comments rising from around US$0.7104 to a high of US$0.7128. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18:13 ET - \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2  will continue with the strategy it announced on Jan. 26, a cornerstone of which is returning at least $25 billion of capital to shareholders over the next two years, according to a person familiar with the insurer's agreement to give \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carl Icahn\f28 \uc2 \f2 's firm and fellow billionaire investor \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Paulson\f28 \uc2 \f2  board seats. \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expanding its board to 16 from 14. Icahn and Paulson have been calling for the immediate breakup of \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2 's approach, instead, is to use selected divestitures, increased cost-cutting and an international reorganization to create "a leaner, more profitable and focused \f28 \uc2 \f2 AIG\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," as AIG CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peter Hancock\f28 \uc2 \f2  has put it. (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 11, 2016 19:35 ET (00:35 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160212ec2c0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\