\par\par \b \uc2 Did the BoJ Act to Weaken the Yen? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2016\par 16:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:46 ET - There is speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  knocked the yen down a bit, helping the dollar pare its losses against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from around Y110.90 to the Y112 level where it trades now. Not everyone agrees, however. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely view such a move as "a waste of money," says Masaato Yanagiya, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo Mitsui in New York. Although the yen has gained nearly 8% against the buck this month alone, the bulk of the move stems from investors' desire to get out of the dollar amid fears of a slowdown in the US and abroad. Yanagiya says knocking down the yen temporarily would do nothing to allay those fears and not stem the flow of money into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 ET - The odds of a recession in the next 12 months has risen to 21%, according to WSJ's monthly survey of economists. Though it's true dismal scientists haven't precisely predicted the timing and contours of recent downturns, their collective assessment of recession odds rose alarmingly in the year before the last one, reaching about 40% by the end of 2007. Odds, however, have also risen without a recession following; they got to 33% amid the debt-ceiling crisis and S&P downgrade of the US in 2011. Odds got up to 24% a year later during the European debt crisis. (josh.zumbrun@wsj.com; @JoshZumbrun)\par \par \uc2 10:41 ET - Every sector of the S&P 500 Index is now down this morning except one: telecommunications services. And it's up more than 2% as the S&P 500 Index remains down more than 1.4%. What gives? Well, it's a small sector of the market, with just five stocks, and one of them happens to be up more than 8%. CenturyLink (CTL), a telephone and internet provider, is boosting the sector after issuing earnings guidance for the 1Q that beat Wall Street guesses. Its earnings also rose sharply. (ben.eisen@wsj.com; @beneisen)\par \par \uc2 10:39 ET - Participants in WSJ's monthly survey of economists trim their GDP and employment forecasts, with 2016 economic growth now seen at 2.3%, versus 2.5% last month and 2.8% a year ago. Meanwhile, they on average now see employment rising 179K/month, versus 190K in January and 199K in December. The only good news is that most economists have held the line on unemployment, which is seen falling to 4.7% by year-end. (josh.zumbrun@wsj.com; @JoshZumbrun)\par \par \uc2 10:33 ET - The odds of a recession in the next 12 months rose to 21%, according to the average estimate of forecasters in the WSJ's monthly survey of economists. While it's true that economists have not precisely predicted the timing and contours of recent recessions, their collective assessment of recession odds rose alarmingly in the year before the most recent recession which officially began in 2007, reaching about 40% by the end of the year. The odds, however, have also risen without a recession following. In 2011 the US debt ceiling crisis and the subsequent downgrade of US bonds led to months of financial turmoil and deteriorating economic data--recession odds rose to 33% during that episode. In 2012, the European debt crisis threatened to seep across the Atlantic and infect the US--recession odds rose as high as 24%. But in both of those episodes, the Fed eventually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economy muddled through. (josh.zumbrun@wsj.com; @JoshZumbrun)\par \par \uc2 10:31 ET - Banks in the S&P 500 are down 2.9%, compared with a 2.2% drop in the benchmark. Financials are the worst-performing sector this morning. It's a theme we've seen all year, and one that doesn't seem to be going away soon. Bank stocks were steady gainers during the years preceding the Fed's first rate increase, since banks were thought to benefit from rising rates. But long-term rates have fallen sharply this year, flattening the yield curve that helps determine net interest margins. The 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  note yield is down sharply again today, trading at a yield of 1.63% and flattening the yield curve again. Plus, with Yellen striking a somewhat dovish tone in regards to future rate hikes, the outlook for long-term rates is cloudy at best. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC) is down 5.2%, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) is down 5.1%, and \uc2 KeyCorp\uc2  (KEY) is down 4.2%. (benjamin.eisen@wsj.com; @beneisen)\par \par \uc2 10:29 ET - Participants in the Wall Street Journal's monthly survey of economists have trimmed their forecasts for US GDP growth and for expansion of payrolls. Estimates of GDP growth in 2016 fell to 2.3% this month from 2.5% last month and 2.8% a year ago. Payrolls are expected to grow by about 179,000 a month this year, down from an estimate of 190,000 in last month's survey and 199,000 in December. The only good news is that most economists have held the line on their unemployment rate forecasts and expect further progress this year. The average forecaster expects the jobless rate to drop to 4.7% by year end. (josh.Zumbrun@wsj.com, @JoshZumbrun)\par \par \uc2 10:29 ET - The CEO of grain merchant Bunge (BG) says double-digit earnings growth is "not impossible" for 2016 after 2015's net income climbed 53% despite recent turbulence afflicting the global grain trade. \uc2 Soren Schroder\uc2  notes anticipation of another massive crop in South America benefits \uc2 BG\uc2 's trading-and-processing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  there while conditions in the sugar business are the best in some time and fertilizer profitability is improving. "We have the potential, the footprint to have a very good year under the right circumstances." But \uc2 BG\uc2  skids 11% to $51.87, hitting a 5 1/2-year low, 4Q results undershot analysts' expectations. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 10:26 ET - Not long ago a bright spot for drug maker Mylan (MYL), top-selling product EpiPen is now a subject of worry for analysts. Its 4Q sales rose 5% to $254.1M, disappointing some after a big rival had to withdraw a product. While MYL sold more of the allergic-reaction treatment, "they clearly are not getting the price Mylan previously suggested they could achieve," says Bernstein & Co. MYL raised EpiPen's list price 15% in 4Q, but executives said during yesterday's conference call that payors weren't willing to pay up. That "creates some cause for concern regarding ongoing pricing pressure on the product," says Citi. (jonathan.rockoff@wsj.com; @jonathanrockoff)\par \par \uc2 10:21 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often cited as a M&A driver: logic suggests the weaker Canadian dollar against its US counterpart would discourage Canadian companies from deal-making in the US by making targets more expensive, while spurring US companies to eye Canadian deals for the opposite reason. But in 4Q when the Canadian dollar weakened 4% against the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Canadian firms doing deals abroad exceeded foreign companies acquiring in Canada by a factor of 1.6 times, according to data from Crosbie & Co. Rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , corporate strategy, overall market sentiment and access to capital drive deals, says Crosbie Managing director Richard Betsalel. (ben.dummett@wsj.com; @bendummett)\par \par \uc2 10:20 ET - \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  (F), which for the last year has been touting itself as an automotive and mobility company, made the designation official in its annual report for 2015, adding the words "mobility company" to the description of its business. The latest regulatory filing also broadens the company's explanation of itself to include a line that says: "We are aggressively pursuing emerging opportunities through Smart Mobility, our plan to be a leader in connectivity, mobility, autonomous vehicles, the customer experience and data and analytics." Also added is a paragraph about the company's efforts to expand into so-called "transportation services" without further elaboration. (christina.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:17 ET - There are multiple bills pending in Congress which would make changes to the way the Fed operates, none of which have exactly been welcomed warmly by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leadership. Sen. Richard Shelby (R., Ala.), chairman of the Senate Banking Committee, today warns the Fed not to reject change simply for the sake of rejecting change. "At times, it seems that Fed officials resist even sensible reforms designed to improve economic performance, congressional oversight or public understanding of the Fed's actions," he said in a prepared opening statement before hearing testimony from Yellen. "The need to preserve Fed independence is very real, but surely it does not justify objection to any reform. Independence and accountability should not be viewed as mutually exclusive concepts." (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 11, 2016 10:46 ET (15:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160211ec2b002jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
