\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen surge prompts speculation BoJ may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2016\par 14:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Yen up across board, now best week vs dollar since 2008\par \par \uc2 * Gives back some gains on speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  & China both shut for public holidays\par \par \uc2 * Sweden cuts rates deep into negative territory (Updates after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries knock yen back)\par \par \uc2 By Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 11 (Reuters) - The yen racked up its strongest weekly gains since the 2008 financial crisis on Thursday before recoiling sharply on speculation officials may soon step in to stem a flood of capital seeking the traditional safety of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Dealers with major banks played down speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  had already stepped in mid-morning in Europe after a two-minute spike in the dollar from 112 to 113 yen.\par \par \uc2 A Japanese government source gave no comment on whether Tokyo had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the middle of a national holiday and dealers said trading volumes were too low to suggest it had.\par \par \uc2 "The volumes in that spike were less than 1 million per tick so there is no evidence in that of an official bid," said a trader with one Asia-focused bank.\par \par \uc2 A chaotic week on European and U.S. stock markets has sent investors scuttling for the perceived safety of the yen, upping its value against the dollar by almost 9 percent since Feb. 1.\par \par \uc2 That is the strongest performance by the yen since the aftermath of the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008 and makes a mockery of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's shock move at the end of January to weaken it with negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "We got to 8 a.m. this morning and it just hit in a wave," said Richard Benson, head of portfolio management at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund Millennium in London.\par \par \uc2 He said the surge was probably driven by model flow from big fund investors adjusting to the sharp moves on stock markets of the last few days.\par \par \uc2 The euro and Swiss franc also benefited, hitting their highest in around four months against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With Japanese markets closed, overall volumes in the yen were half of those a day earlier on the EBS trading platform, which some warned could be exaggerating the scale of the moves.\par \par \uc2 But the concern over the world's growth prospects and the solidity of its banks is palpable. European stock markets were down another 3 percent and have lost 28 percent from peaks last April. Shares in \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  fell 9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  was the latest to respond, cutting interest rates further into negative territory and by more than analysts had expected, driving its crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than 1 percent lower before it staged a recovery.\par \par \uc2 Analysts from a number of major banks have raised the prospect this week of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as it last did in October 2011. Such a move could be a hard sell to global policy forums like the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and G20, which generally discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It looks like we may head back to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that is also hard to justify and may ultimately fail," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  analysts said in a note on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Market thinking and moves in the last 24 hours have been dominated by Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , whose testimony to Congress on Wednesday gave investors no reason to change their minds that the next rate hike will be a long time coming.\par \par \uc2 Sticking largely to the script, Yellen made clear that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained on a path of 'gradual' policy tightening, although she also highlighted growing risks facing the economy.\par \par \uc2 That gave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors the green light to continue the current trading theme: buying the safe-haven yen.\par \par \uc2 By 1334 GMT, the dollar was 0.9 percent lower at 112.38 yen , having briefly reached 110.99 yen.\par \par \uc2 The euro last traded at $1.1324, up a third of a percent on the day. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more than 1 percent higher against sterling at 78.68 pence but 0.6 percent down against the yen at 127.28. (Additional reporting by Jemima Kelly; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-11T14:58:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160211ec2b00ies\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
