\par\par \b \uc2 *Japan Fin Min Aso: Yen's Movement Clearly Rough\b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2016\par 00:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8 Feb 2016 18:49 ET *Japan Fin Min Aso: Will Continue to Closely Watch Yen\par \par \uc2 8 Feb 2016 18:53 ET *Japan Fin Min Aso: Must Keep Monitoring Impact of BOJ Negative Rate Policy on Fincl Institutions\par \par \uc2 8 Feb 2016 19:04 ET  Japan Finance Minister Warns Over 'Rough' Moves in Yen\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said Tuesday that the yen's appreciation overnight was clearly sharp, indicating the government's discomfort over a rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that could undermine a sluggish economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The markets have shown rough movements lately. That is clear," Mr. Aso said at a regular news conference, in response to a question about the yen's gains overnight. "I am going to continue to closely watch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to Y115.17 in New York trading Monday, the lowest in more than a year, as a U.S.-stock-market rout drove investors into Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other assets generally seen as safe investments. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent drop has been dramatic considering its brief rise to above Y121 late last month after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's additional monetary stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rises in the yen make Japanese exports less competitive and reduce the value of repatriated profits, a grim prospect for Tokyo policy makers who have counted on Japan's economy escaping from a lull on the back of high earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Aso is in charge of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy and has the final say over whether to have the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to influence the yen. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in late 2011 to buy the dollar in order to arrest its then-persistent falls versus the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 08, 2016 19:04 ET (00:04 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160208ec28003lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
