\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Hits One Year High Against Dollar -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2016\par 22:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen strengthened broadly Wednesday and reached the strongest level in a year against the dollar as a deepening rout in crude oil and weaker global stocks drove investors to protect capital.\par \par \uc2 The yen, like \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds and gold, has long been perceived as a haven asset class. That means it typically attracts buyers when sentiment sours on riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's gain added to the yen's price rally this month. Worries over a slowdown in China's economy have caused a big drop in China's stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which rippled globally. The weekslong weakness in stocks amid rising volatility, along with a continued decline in crude oil prices, has jolted investors' confidence over the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "We are in a risk-aversion atmosphere, and the yen is a winner when investors cut risks," said Brian Daingerfield, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Securities.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar at one point dropped to Yen115.97, the lowest level against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since January 2015.\par \par \uc2 In late-afternoon trading, the dollar recouped some ground as U.S. stocks cut losses. One dollar bought Yen116.895, compared with Yen117.635 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro softened against the dollar ahead of the policy meeting from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. One euro bought $1.0887, compared with $1.0910 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro has dropped against the dollar in the past two years driven by the divergent paths of interest rate policy between the U.S. and the eurozone.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates in December for the first time since 2006 and signaled more tightening to come this year. \uc2 The ECB\uc2  implemented additional monetary stimulus in December. Higher interest rates typically attract investors to buy a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for better returns.\par \par \uc2 But this month through Tuesday, the euro was up 0.45% against the dollar. Shaun Osborne, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2 , said the stock market weakness has pushed investors to reduce short bets against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Osborne expects \uc2 the ECB\uc2  to hold steady Thursday. He said that further accommodation policy from \uc2 the ECB\uc2  is still a possibility later this year. "I don't think [ECB President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 ] will welcome the recent strength in the euro, so there is a risk that he tries to talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower," he said.\par \par \uc2 The yen has long been an indicator of investors' risk appetites. For a long time, investors have been borrowing the yen due to the nation's ultralow interest rates, then using the proceeds to buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stocks and bonds in many other countries. It is known as the carry trade because investors can profit from the rate differentials.\par \par \uc2 When markets become volatile, investors are under growing pressure to cut back risk. One way to do that is to dial back the trade by buying back the yen and selling many other assets.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, the unwinding rattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Australia and New Zealand to Brazil--many have long been investors' favorites for the carry trade because of their higher interest rates. At the peak of the selling during Wednesday morning's session, the Australian dollar, the New Zealand dollar and the Brazil real all fell by over 1%.\par \par \uc2 "That sign of [unwinding yen-funded investments] has been clearly visible since the onset of the new year," said John Sidawi, portfolio manager at Federated Investors Inc., which had $350 billion in assets under management as of December.\par \par \uc2 Mazen Issa, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 , said he expects the dollar to fall to Yen110 before the end of June. He advised clients to buy the yen against the dollar and some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's broad strength might be a problem for Japan. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes a nation's exports less competitive in global trade and hurts Japanese firms' earnings outlook. Exports play a large part in Japan's economy, which has been mired in lackluster growth for years despite monetary stimulus from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the BOJ were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this environment, all it would end up doing is providing the market with better levels to buy yen," said Mr. Sidawi. "I think the BOJ is much smarter than that, and knows that the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now would be blunted in this deleveraging environment."\par \par \uc2 Other money managers say a moderate gain in the yen isn't going to be a problem for Japan given that the yen has been falling sharply over the past few years. That means even with an uptick, many still see the yen as relatively cheap. The dollar has been up 56.44% over the last four years against the yen through the end of 2015.\par \par \uc2 Write to Min Zeng at min.zeng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-01-16 2148GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160120ec1k000jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
