\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan MOF names Masatsugu Asakawa as top financial diplomat\b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2015\par 03:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Appointment is part of routine personnel reshuffle               \par \par \uc2 * Asakawa likely to stick with G7, G20 stance on markets               \par \par \uc2 * Asakawa close to Finance Minister Aso   (Adds background on Asakawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  record)               \par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto               \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said international policy veteran Masatsugu Asakawa will become the country's top financial diplomat, whose tasks include coordinating with other nations and the \uc2 IMF\uc2  as well as arranging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Asakawa's broad network with policymakers both inside and outside Japan puts him in an ideal position to coordinate with the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and other nations at a time when the Greek debt crisis threatens to ripple through the world economy.               \par \par \uc2 The appointment of Asakawa on Tuesday also comes as concerns have grown that a further rapid depreciation in the yen could damage Japanese households and small firms by raising import costs, while dollar gains hurt U.S. exporters.               \par \par \uc2 Asakawa, 57, who has been director-general of the finance ministry's international bureau, will take over from Tatsuo Yamasaki, who will retire. His appointment is part of a routine reshuffle and is not expected to bring about a shift in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.               \par \par \uc2 The new vice finance minister for international affairs is likely to stick with the G7 and G20 commitment to market-set exchange rates and a policy that does not target \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asakawa currently chairs the OECD Committee on Fiscal Affairs, dealing with an anti-tax avoidance action plan known as the anti-BEPS (Base Erosion, Profit Shifting) directive.               \par \par \uc2 Asakawa is close to Finance Minister Taro Aso, having served as executive secretary when Aso was premier in 2008-2009, and as executive assistant when he became finance minister in 2012.               \par \par \uc2 Asakawa joined the ministry in 1981. His previous posts include director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division for two years from July 2004. He also served as deputy vice minister for policy planning and coordination in 2013-14, liaising with the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in November 2011 to stem a strong yen. In 1998, it bought the yen to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when the country was grappling with the Asian financial crisis and its own banking crisis.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-7-7T02:17:34.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150707eb77001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
