\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Kuroda Indicates No Intention to Stem Sliding Yen With Recent Remark -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2015\par 07:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Bank of Japan Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda on Tuesday clarified his recent controversial remark on the yen, saying he had no intention to evaluate exchange-rate levels or project the future direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest comment from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief comes the week after he caused the dollar to tumble versus the yen by telling parliament that a technical measure of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value---known as the real effective rate---was unlikely to weaken further.\par \par \uc2 "In making those comments I was neither trying to assess the nominal exchange rate (of the yen) nor forecast its future movement," Mr. Kuroda said Tuesday during a session of the upper house financial affairs committee.\par \par \uc2 The latest remarks caused a brief jump in the dollar, but not on the scale of movement seen last week.\par \par \uc2 In the face of grilling from opposition lawmakers, Mr. Kuroda also retracted his forecast on the real-effective rate. Projecting its direction is difficult considering the multiple elements influencing its levels, such as differences in inflation and trade conditions among economies, he said. The real effective rate also is a theoretical idea that doesn't necessarily indicate where the yen's regular exchange rate will go, Mr. Kuroda added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda's clarification indicates that Japan is generally tolerant of the weakening yen, which benefits the country's export-driven economy, although officials are keen to discourage rapid exchange-rate movements.\par \par \uc2 "It is desirable for the exchange rate to move stably in reflection of economic fundamentals, and as long as that condition is being met, (the yen's decline) will have no adverse effects on the overall economy," Mr. Kuroda said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the dollar rose on the latest comments to Y123.81 from Y123.35, before settling down around Y123.56.\par \par \uc2 The movement Tuesday and the sharp market reaction seen last week underline how influential Mr. Kuroda's opinions are over the yen's direction.\par \par \uc2 When the BOJ governor spoke last week, some traders thought Tokyo was starting to check the yen's slide, despite the absence of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by finance minister Taro Aso, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy maker. Others suspected that Mr. Kuroda might be reluctant to further expand the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing program, which is widely seen as a main cause of the yen's downtrend versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A person close to the government told The Wall Street Journal that Mr. Kuroda's remarks last week were "careless" and didn't represent the view of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's administration.\par \par \uc2 --Hiroyuki Kachi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 16, 2015 01:25 ET (05:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150616eb6g000gm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
