\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Beat: Did Market Misunderstand Kuroda's Yen Remarks?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2015\par 12:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Many people were caught off guard when the yen began soaring during trading in Asia Wednesday, reacting to what some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders interpreted as a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda. Among them: central bankers themselves.\par \par \uc2 Aggressive yen buying began the moment Mr. Kuroda spoke of an obscure measure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value during testimony before parliament. The yen's real-effective rate, as the measure is known, was very weak relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and unlikely to fall further, Mr. Kuroda said. The following 15 minutes saw the yen climb sharply, sending the dollar down by roughly one and a half yen to Yen123.\par \par \uc2 Some BOJ officials were surprised, both by the abrupt move in the market and how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief's words were interpreted, according to people close to the BOJ. Mr. Kuroda himself was puzzled, they said, by the dramatic market reaction his remarks had set off.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts thought that Japan was finally beginning efforts to arrest the yen's decline, after two and a half years of a downturn, which had accelerated in recent weeks. Others speculated that Mr. Kuroda might avoid adding to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive easing measures out of fears that it could cause the yen's further drop.\par \par \uc2 But "there was no such intention at all" in Mr. Kuroda's comments, one of the people close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. Rather, he said, the market "misunderstood" him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda's remarks were neither about the regular exchange rate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market nor about the yen's short-term prospects, this person explained. He stressed that the comments had "zero" implications for monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda's comments were off the cuff in nature, rather than a well-contemplated presentation, the people said. Indeed, the issue of the real exchange rate came out of the blue for the governor while responding to questions from Seiji Maehara, an opposition lawmaker who is known to disregard the widely followed parliamentary custom of submitting questions ahead of time, they said.\par \par \uc2 A former top finance ministry official in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Mr. Kuroda well understands the consequences of speaking rashly about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. But at the same time, he is known to be a policy wonk and an avid reader of academic papers. Being thrown such a technical question, Mr. Kuroda was perhaps unable to resist the temptation to speak "like an economist," one of the people said, forgetting for a moment to act like a policy maker. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 12, 2015 06:30 ET (10:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150612eb6c00147\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
