\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan finmin Aso says yen slide "rough," will watch closely\b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2015\par 19:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments and details)\par \par \uc2 DRESDEN, Germany, May 28 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso on Thursday fired a verbal warning shot against the yen's slide, saying that the decline in recent days had been "rough" and he would monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets carefully.\par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed to a 12-1/2-year high against the yen earlier on Thursday as investors bet that U.S. interest rates would rise later this year while Japanese monetary policy remains ultra-loose.\par \par \uc2 "The current yen weakening in the past few days has been rough. I will closely monitor market moves," Aso told reporters on the sidelines of a gathering of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the Group of Seven countries in Germany.\par \par \uc2 The remark was stronger than recent mild warnings by Japanese policymakers against excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turbulence, cautioning markets against pushing the yen down too rapidly.\par \par \uc2 Japan's export-reliant economy has historically suffered from a strong yen, so many policymakers have welcomed limited yen declines.\par \par \uc2 But some have begun to worry about the drawbacks, such as boosting import costs and eroding consumers' purchasing power, which could outweigh benefits to exporters.\par \par \uc2 Aso said G7 finance leaders did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Thursday's gathering.\par \par \uc2 But Aso said he had reaffirmed the G7/G20 commitment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when meeting U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew.\par \par \uc2 G7 and G20 major economies are committed to market-determined exchange rates.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in November 2011, to stem a strengthening yen. It last bought the yen to arrest its decline versus the dollar in the late 1990s, when the country was grappling with the Asian financial crisis and its own banking crisis. (Writing by William Schomberg and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Andrew Roche) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-5-28T18:58:54.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150528eb5s00pgp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
