\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Chinese Equities Fall Out of Bed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alen Mattich   \par \uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2015\par 13:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP : Chinese equities fell out of bed overnight, dropping 6.5% after a unit of the country's sovereign wealth fund cut its stakes in China's biggest state-owned banks. But after a 50%-plus rally since the start of the year to where many firms are on extreme valuations something was bound to trip up the Chinese market. Not that the Chinese bubble is likely to fully deflate as long as the government keeps the fiscal and monetary taps on. Longer term, it's questionable how much good policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do the economy - just look at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest retail sales numbers. Elsewhere, Greek negotiations continue to play themselves out. For all that it sometimes runs like a farce, the outcome could yet prove tragic. (AM)\par \par \uc2 GREECE: Greek government sources are reported as saying that a deal with the country's creditors is close at hand. \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governing council member \uc2 Ewald Nowotny\uc2  says that interim financing to keep Greece afloat wouldn't be legal.\par \par \uc2 Greek negotiations continue in the fine traditions of pantomime performance. A deal is at hand. Oh no it's not. What is clear is that there isn't much time for the Greeks to come up with the money to pay back the next slice of what they owe \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . The Greek government says it hasn't got the resources at home, which is why Greece is calling on its creditors. How much ground will either party give? And is there even enough time left to do a deal and get it passed by Greek legislators? (AM)\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE: May \uc2 European Commission\uc2  economic sentiment indicator was unchanged at 103.8 against a long run average of 100.0.\par \par \uc2 A soft euro, plenty of \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity and signs of a pickup in growth helped to keep eurozone sentiment at its most positive since mid-2011. In the detail, businesses were slightly more confident and households a little less so. The survey offers hope that business investment will start to pick up leading to a self-sustaining rebound. (AM)\par \par \uc2 U.K.: First-quarter gross domestic product growth remained unrevised at 0.3% on the previous quarter and 1.2% on an annualized basis.\par \par \uc2 Slowing service sector growth and a widening trade deficit meant that earlier estimates of first-quarter U.K. GDP growth were left unrevised against expectations of a small uptick. Signs that business investment is picking up offered some encouragement for the economy's prospects. But \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policy makers will be looking for productivity to start picking up as well, otherwise they face the uncomfortable prospect of having to increase rates in an environment of muted growth. (AM)\par \par \uc2 SWITZERLAND: April exports fell 1.3% on the year and imports fell 0.7% while the trade surplus widened to CHF2.9 billion from CHF2.5 billion in March.\par \par \uc2 The strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dented Swiss exports, though weak commodity prices helped to drive down imports. Nonetheless, Switzerland still managed to generate a substantial trade surplus, highlighting the underlying strength of the economy's manufactures. One question is whether the FIFA scandal manages to dent sentiment about doing business in the country. (AM)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN: April retail sales rose 5.0% on the year.\par \par \uc2 Last year's sales tax rise has made it hard to get a true flavor of how Japanese consumption might be developing. Then, households ramped up purchases in March, ahead of the tax rise, and cut them in April when the increase came through. As a result the year on year change in sales dropped 9.7% the previous month and snapped back this month. The government's assessment is that retails sales have essentially been flat, which doesn't say much for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's massive stimulus program. (AM)\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINES: First-quarter GDP expanded 5.2% on the year against median forecasts of 6.4% and below the government's 7% to 8% target.\par \par \uc2 P hilippine economic growth dropped to its slowest pace since the final quarter of 2011 on weakness in agricultural output, though services and consumption were also fairly subdued. Still, investors remain hopeful. Philippine equities have managed to outpace some of its regional neighbors like Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand since the start of the year. (AM)\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA: First-quarter business investment fell 4.4% on the quarter against expectations of a 2.2% fall.\par \par \uc2 Australian companies, led by the miners, are cutting their forecast investment programs by a quarter from what they'd expected earlier. \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already chopped interest rates to record lows to shore the growth up. But rescue might have to come from further afield. Will China's stimulus program help to underpin the economy? (AM)\par \par \uc2 To sign up for this email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 28, 2015 07:14 ET (11:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150528eb5s001ck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
