\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2  says China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy should continue to allow more flexibility\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2015\par 03:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 7 (Reuters) - China should allow greater flexibility in its exchange rate policy by reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its efforts to secure a gradual moderation in growth while pursuing economic reforms, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "To help deliver durable and balanced growth, China needs reforms that reorient the economy away from excessive reliance on real estate, heavy industry, and external demand," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said in its regional economic outlook for Asia and the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Implementation of China's third plenum reform blueprint of 2013, including steps to facilitate corporate deleveraging, strengthen local government financial discipline and reforms to state-owned enterprises, is vital for sustainable growth, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 As such reforms are fully implemented, China's growth is expected to moderate to 6 percent by 2017 and then stabilise around there, it said.\par \par \uc2 In order to achieve a gradual moderation in growth while pursuing reforms, "fiscal stimulus should be the first line of defense, with an emphasis on measures to support private consumption," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said, adding that any further monetary easing in China should be data dependent.\par \par \uc2 As the economy cools, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has delivered two interest rate cuts since November, on top of two reductions in the amount of money banks must keep in reserve, and repeated attempts to reduce financing costs.\par \par \uc2 "Exchange rate policy should continue to allow greater flexibility by reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said an aggressive policy response would be key to achieving "escape velocity" for the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 "Further enhancements to the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's monetary stimulus may be warranted, particularly to the 'qualitative' part of monetary easing, if necessary to achieve the 2 percent price stability target," it said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ last week pushed back the timeframe for hitting its inflation target but refrained from expanding its already massive stimulus programme, clinging to its conviction that a steady economic recovery will gradually nudge up prices.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said the BOJ could take steps such as lengthening the maturity of Japanese government bonds it buys, and could also strengthen its forward guidance by committing to maintaining the size of its balance sheet even after exiting its quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider accelerating qualitative and quantitative easing if the drop in oil prices affects core inflation or longer-term inflation expectations," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 While Japan's decision to postpone a second increase in the consumption tax to April 2017 was "appropriate" given the uncertain growth outlook, a credible strategy for medium-term fiscal consolidation is needed, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  also noted that India's economy has made a remarkable turnaround since mid-2013.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should "maintain its tight monetary policy stance" the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said, adding that elevated inflation expectations and the possibility of supply-side shocks will continue to challenge the achievement of India's medium-term inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  kept interest rates on hold at 7.50 percent at its policy review in early April, after having cut interest rates twice this year by a total 50 basis points to bolster the economy. But it is widely expected to cut rates again before the end of June. (Reporting by Masayuki Kitano; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-5-7T02:30:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150507eb57001ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
