\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2  Chief Taps Japan's Former Top  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Official As a New Lieutenant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley   \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2015\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  announced last month that Naoyuki Shinohara was stepping down as one of the fund's cadre of deputy managing directors, some in Tokyo might have worried Japan was losing key influence within the world's emergency lender.\par \par \uc2 But they had little to fear: The \uc2 IMF\uc2 's largest lender will keep a top spot in the fund.\par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  on Thursday said she planned to appoint Japan's former top international financial diplomat, \uc2 Mitsuhiro Furusawa\uc2 , as one of her new lieutenants.\par \par \uc2 As Japan revs up its monetary-easing machine, Tokyo will have an official in the fund who has defended the yen's rapid depreciation in recent years. Mr. Furusawa played a key role as vice finance minister in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the government's decisions on whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 He'll also enter an \uc2 IMF\uc2  where management has a much more sanguine view about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuations around the world than the fund has in the past.\par \par \uc2 It's not Mr. Furusawa's first time at the fund. From 2010 to 2012, he represented his country's interests on the fund's 24-member executive board. An alum of France's famous ENA, the \u201\'00\'C9cole Nationale d'Administration, he's a well-known figure in international circles.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2 , which is supposed to be the world's arbiter for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy violations, has largely backed the depreciation of the yen and the euro as the U.S. dollar has strengthened to decade-high records. As a host of other central banks around the world also crank up their \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus, which also depreciates their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 IMF\uc2  argues the resulting exchange-rate volatility is a natural byproduct of the interest-rate and growth schisms around the world.\par \par \uc2 But while \uc2 IMF\uc2  officials and President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's administration may be relatively unconcerned about the current round of international exchange-rate devaluations, some in the U.S. aren't so happy.\par \par \uc2 A new report published Wednesday by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a left-leaning think tank, underscored the growing concern that many U.S.-based manufacturers have about a strong dollar undermining their export competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 "As one of the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, Japan is responsible for a substantial share of the bloated U.S. global trade deficit," said \uc2 Robert Scott\uc2 , the group's director of trade research.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  appears to be honoring its \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  pledge to "remain oriented towards meeting our respective domestic objectives using domestic instruments."\par \par \uc2 But Japan's Government Pension Investment Fund has bulked up its foreign assets in its portfolio to $1.7 trillion, according to Mr. Scott, "holdings that will continue to suppress the value of the Japanese yen."\par \par \uc2 Japan's dollar buying risks making it the top holder of \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  securities, overtaking China's long-held position.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is one of the political hurdles facing the Obama administration as it tries to get congressional approval for is trade agenda.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Furusawa's appointment--giving Japan an official at the fund who has defended a weaker yen--may give some in the U.S. another cause for concern. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 05, 2015 19:30 ET (00:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150206eb260003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
