\par\par \b \uc2 Foreign Net Buy Orders on 600,000 Japan Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2015\par 00:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Offshore accounts at 6 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 600,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders. The fourth consecutive day of premarket buying may serve as a mild positive for the market at the open, although the yen value basis of the figures is unknown. Buy orders total 7.4 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 6.8 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/JPY is likely to trade in a lower range Monday, undermined by flows to haven JPY amid increased risk aversion as exit polls showing Greece's antiausterity leftist Syriza party leading in national elections Sunday heightened fears that Greece could eventually exit the eurozone. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics neutral. Support at 117.25-117.18 band (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low); breach would target 116.92 (Jan. 19 low), then 115.85 (Jan. 16 low). Resistance at 117.95 (hourly chart), then at 118.59 (hourly chart) and 118.82-118.87 (Friday's high-Tuesday's high); breach would target 119.32 (Jan. 12 high). Spot USD/JPY is at 117.35. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The euro is lower early in Asia trade (the EUR/USD now at 1.1131, compared with 1.1208 late Friday in New York) ahead of final Greek election results, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market doesn't show turbulence, says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup Global Markets Japan\uc2  in a morning note. "The market seemed to have been considerably well prepared" for the Greek election. Rather than to expect a quick tumble in the euro following the election results later today, "it would be appropriate to expect a trend of moderate weakness in the EUR will continue" in keeping with the progress in the ECB's bond purchase and falloff in bond yields in the eurozone. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Nymex crude is likely to trade with risks skewed to the downside near term after settling down 72 cents at $45.59/bbl Friday, \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bullish, but the slow stochastic measure has reverted to bearish mode, while the five-day moving average is turning downward below the falling 15-day moving average. Support is at $44.20 (near six-year low hit Jan. 13); breach would expose downside to $43.83 (April 21, 2009, reaction low), then to the lower 20-day two-standard deviation Bollinger Band (now at $42.81), the psychological $40.00 line and $39.44 (March 3, 2009, low). But a rise above $47.76 (Friday's high) would temper the negative near-term view, exposing upside to $49.09 (Thursday's high, near the middle 20-day Bollinger Band), then to $49.20 (Tuesday's high), $51.27 (Jan. 15 high), $52.73 (Jan. 5 high) and $55.11 (Jan. 2 high). March crude is down 91 cents at $44.68/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT - U.S. stock futures opened lower on Sunday evening after Greece's leftist, antiausterity Syriza party was projected to win the country's election, spurring fears that Greece could exit the eurozone. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were trading 107 points lower, at 17481, a decline of 0.61%. Meanwhile, the euro lost ground against the dollar, falling 0.62% to $1.1137, according to recent FactSet data. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The EUR/USD will likely trade in a 1.11-1.12 range in Asia trade, with the pair already coming under pressure on early indications of the Greek election, says Akira Moroga, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  products group at \uc2 Aozora Bank\uc2 . Already experiencing massive selling to date, the pair (now at 1.1142) could bounce back on covering of short positions by investors once the election results are out. That may prevent the pair from falling below 1.11 temporarily, but "the next target is 1.10 as an underlying trend." The latest official projection shows Greek antiausterity leftist party Syriza winning, but it may not have enough support to form its own government. Ahead of the closely watched FOMC meeting, the USD/JPY is tipped in a 117.00-118.00 range in a directionless trade. The pair (now at 117.64) likely will remain top heavy on lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan stocks are likely to weaken Monday, after Wall Street shares were mostly lower Friday, while both the dollar and the euro are down sharply versus the yen. In Greece's closely watched elections, the nation's incumbent prime minister conceded defeat, while exit polls reported by state television showed the antiausterity Syriza party poised to win. "Stock markets rallied late last week in the wake of the ECB's bigger-than-expected quantitative easing program, but the signs that are appearing around Greece's elections are throwing renewed doubt on the viability of the euro," says Investrust CEO \uc2 Hiroyuki Fukunaga\uc2 . "As the political landscape reshapes in Greece, attention is also on corporate earnings (in both the U.S. and Japan, as well as on the Fed (which meets January 27-28); investors are keen to know if the outlook for the next rate hike (expected by many by June) will be changed at all." Fukunaga puts the Nikkei's range for the session at 17250 to 17500. Nikkei 225 March futures ended Friday's Chicago trading down 150 points at 17410 versus their close earlier in Osaka at 17510. in the cash market, the Nikkei closed up 1.1% at 17511.75 Friday. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:58 EST - Many investors have believed the Fed would do anything to keep stocks stable, which reinforced demand for stocks, according to The Wall Street Journal's Abreast of the Market column. Lately, Fed officials have tried to dispel that notion, leading investors to conclude that the Fed would tolerate sharper market swings, although it wasn't going to let financial markets collapse again. "While volatility has picked up recently, it is still below average," notes investment strategist \uc2 Edgar Peters\uc2  at \uc2 First Quadrant LP\uc2 . Broad market indexes historically rise or fall 1% on about one-third of trading days, he says. With inflation low and the Fed planning only gradual rate increases, volatility won't get back to normal immediately, Peters says. (jim.browning@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:57 EST - \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's evolution away from easy-money policies isn't the only cause of market volatility. Another big factor is that other leading central banks aren't following in the Fed's footsteps. As the Fed reduces its economic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are increasing theirs because their economies are soft. The \uc2 Bank of China\uc2 , using different methods, is supporting its economy, whose growth rate is declining. The central banks are pulling the world in different directions. "The divergence in policy is leading to increased volatility," \uc2 Jim McDonald\uc2 , chief investment strategist at \uc2 Northern Trust\uc2 , says in The Wall Street Journal's Abreast of the Market column. (jim.browning@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:57 EST - Fed policy makers meet on Tuesday and Wednesday, and many professional investors expect no change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to push up short-term interest rates later this year. The plan is part of the Fed's gradual removal of unprecedented easy-money policies it adopted to prevent a depression. "We are transitioning from a Fed-specific market to a more-normal regime," \uc2 Stephen Wood\uc2 , chief market strategist at \uc2 Russell Investments\uc2 , says in The Wall Street Journal's Abreast of the Market column. When stocks depend less on Fed support, they move less in lockstep, reacting more to market fundamentals such as corporate earnings. "You are seeing different securities with different fundamentals behaving differently," Wood says. (jim.browning@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 25, 2015 18:57 ET (23:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150125eb1p000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
