\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Authorities Unlikely to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Even if JPY Hits 130 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2015\par 01:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Historical patterns since the early 1990s show Japanese authorities tend to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (with USD-selling, JPY-buying) once the USD/JPY hits 130.90 on average, but \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to do so even assuming the pair comes close to the 130 mark, says Daisaku Ueno, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in a report. "Japanese stocks would very likely fall if USD-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place under the current circumstances," says Ueno. That outcome would contradict PM \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's emphasis on stock price growth, Ueno says. He adds that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MOF at a time when the BOJ is carrying out large-scale quantitative easing would look inconsistent with PM Abe's Abenomics policy of tackling persistent deflation. The USD/JPY is now at 116.10. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The EUR/USD may test the 1.15 mark in Asia trade, says Mizuho Securities chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kengo Suzuki, who tips the pair to trade in a 1.1500-1.1650 range. While the rapid pace of decline in the pair has made it easier for some dip-buying to kick in, the pair could still set new lows Friday, Suzuki says, as Thursday's SNB decision to scrap the CHF cap adds to expectations that the ECB will launch a bond purchase program at next week's policy meeting. Given downward pressure on both the USD/JPY and EUR/JPY, the EUR/JPY now remains susceptible to a fall toward 134. The EUR/JPY is now at 135.36 after falling below 135 (as low as 134.70) for the first time since Oct. 16. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2241 GMT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has downgraded its inflation forecasts for New Zealand as a significant fall in petrol prices in New Zealand is likely to drive lower inflation and pushed out expectations for rate increases, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. The investment bank is now expecting 4Q CPI inflation to be 1.0% vs. a previous forecast of 1.0% on year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds that lower oil prices are lower expected to mean lower CPI inflation for the 2015 as well. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds due to low inflation \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  now expects the RBNZ to keep rates on hold at 3.50% for longer, before lifting the cash rate in Q4 2015 vs. prior forecast of a hike in 3Q 2015. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2159 GMT - Sandfire Resources N.L. (SFR.AU) and, to a lesser extent, \f28 \uc2 \f2 OZ Minerals Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (OZL.AU) look the best placed of Australia's mid-cap copper stocks to weather the storm in global metal markets, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Luke Nelson. "SFR's and OZL's balance sheets look reasonable under spot" prices, he says. On the other hand, Laos-focused miner \f28 \uc2 \f2 PanAust Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (PNA.AU) is facing lower revenue and higher net debt, given its near-term spending plans for the Frieda River project. "Given its financial leverage, and no exposure to a weaker AUD, PNA's valuation is the most impacted from spot pricing," he says. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; @RhiannonHoyle)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:39 EST - Samson Resources bonds lose 13.3% to reach fresh lows of 26c on the dollar. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 KKR\f28 \uc2 \f2 -controlled oil-and-gas producer has struggled under weight of debt taken on in its 2011 leveraged buyout and prices for the oil and natural gas it produces. The 9.75% notes due 2020 have lost 28.8% so far this year. U.S. crude benchmark down 13.2% on the year, including a 4.7% fall Thursday. U.S. natural gas up 8.6% on the year despite a 1.8% fall Thursday. (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:37 EST - Companies are storming the bond market in size on Thursday, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  selling $7.15B and Mexican oil giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  selling $6B, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P Capital IQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  LCD. The deals come as yields on Treasury bonds plummeted on Thursday, lowering borrowing costs for companies, in the wake of a major policy shift from Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . A 2026 bond from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to yield 4.57%, while a 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond is expected to yield around 3.25%, according to LCD. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:30 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's elimination of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange cap on Swiss franc jostled \f28 \uc2 \f2 Syngenta\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares Thursday, but Morningstar analysts see little impact on Basel-based seed and chemical company. That's down to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Syngenta\f28 \uc2 \f2 's cosmopolitan structure: "Vast majority" of sales and costs occur outside Switzerland, its financial statements reported in US dollars, and debt denominated in US dollars and euros, Morningstar says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Syngenta\f28 \uc2 \f2 's European-traded shares settled 8.7% lower at 303.20 Swiss francs, while US-traded American depository receipts close 4.4% higher at $68. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2119 GMT - NZD/EUR has blasted to a new post float higher after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  roiled markets last night by announcing the end of the EUR/CHF floor, ANZ says. "The read through from SNB action is that the SNB expects the ECB to unleash a large scale balance sheet expansion next week, something they clearly felt they couldn't keep up with." ANZ adds that a larger than expected QE from the ECB would support high yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the NZD. The pair is at 0.6737 after touching a high of 0.6746 overnight. ANZ expects the pair to trade in a short term range of 0.6650 to 0.6900. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:14 EST - Low oil prices and investor uncertainty over the Fed's plan to raise interest rates are creating buying opportunities in frontier and emerging markets, says Caglar Somek, chief investment officer of the $750M Caravel Fund. "We're not going to see any decline in volatility until the Fed decides to hike rates and oil prices stabilize," he says, but the uncertainty is driving down valuations across the board, leaving some markets considerably undervalued. Somek says consumption-driven economies will benefit greatly from cheaper oil -- even if prices rise to $60-$70. He's particularly enthusiastic about Pakistan, Bangladesh, the Philippines and Vietnam. "Any shock that puts pressure on these markets would create an amazing buying opportunity," he says. (dan.keeler@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15:38 EST - The Mexican government is throwing its weight behind a U.S. lawsuit filed by the parents of a Mexican teenager who was killed in his country when a U.S. Border Patrol agent fired his weapon across the border. Lawyers for the country said in a court brief Thursday that "it is one of Mexico's priorities to ensure that the United States has provided adequate means to hold the agents accountable and compensate the victims." The case, which is now before the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, is the first to ask whether foreign victims of cross-border shootings have rights under the U.S. Constitution. A ruling is expected later this year. (joe.palazzolo@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15:34 EST - The New Zealand dollar has climbed in the wake of the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's overnight moves versus the greenback after the latter couldn't push below US$0.77. "When investors stepped back last night, the path of least resistance was higher," says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Raiko Shareef. The kiwi got as high as US$0.7885, for now stemming recent weakness. It's currently at US$0.7817 versus US$0.7733 late Thursday in New Zealand. Shareef said BNZ suspects markets will remain volatile heading into the weekend, with the pair trading in a range of US$0.7750-0.7900. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15:31 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  favors Mexican inflation-linked bonds over fixed-rate for all maturities, seeing risks to the inflation outlook despite smaller-than-expected inrceases in gasoline and cuts in electricity rates this year. "Long-term inflation breakevens are at a historically low 3.2%. This implies only 20bp of inflation risk premium (target inflation is 3%), which we consider too little for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that has consistently missed the target in the last 10 years," firm says. The firm raised its 2015 CPI estimate to 3.5% from 3.4%, not taking into account possible further increases in the minimum wage. CPI ended 2014 up 4.1%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 15, 2015 19:16 ET (00:16 GMT)\f28 \par 