\par\par \b \uc2 South Korea Won Hits Eyebrow-Raising 6-Year High Against Yen -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2014\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The rally of South Korea's won Thursday has taken it to a six-year high versus trade rival Japan's yen--which could raise eyebrows at \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 . Prolonged won strength versus the yen could make South Korean exports seem relatively more expensive than those from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOK might spring into action to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thursday's bearish closing for USD/KRW suggests more dollar weakness--thereby won strength--may be ahead. USD/KRW's closing below the 1,100 round-figure trading barrier opens the way to the 20-day Bollinger mid support line at 1,095. If this too breaks, USD/KRW could enter the Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,082. The U.S. dollar slipped against most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after several below-forecast U.S. economic reports released on Wednesday. USD/KRW closed at 1,098.4 versus its Wednesday close of 1,106.5. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY), India's biggest telecommunications company is down 1.5% and is trading 387.25 rupees a piece after Nigeria, raised interest rates and devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nigeria is the largest country by \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Bharti's African unit. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; Twitter: @jaikrishna) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0711 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bajaj Auto (532977.BY) is weak for the second day in a row. Investors are concerned about the ability of the Indian scooter-maker's ability to sustain its export growth after Nigeria, a key market, devalues its local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the niara, and raises benchmark interest rates to offset sharp fall in global oil prices. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation would be inflationary and affect consumer demand in Nigeria and the Indian companies that export to the African country," said Sharekhan. a brokerage, in a report. Bajaj Auto is the market leader in Nigeria with a 40% share, it sold 450,000 motorcycles there in fiscal year 2013-14. The company also has a similar market share in three-wheeled vehicles. Sharekhan expects Bajaj Auto to raise prices to compensate for the niara's devaluation, with the company and dealers also sharing part of the burden of higher costs. "Thus, there would be a negative impact on the sales in volume terms (a 20% growth was seen in the sales volume last year) and the margins of the Nigerian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," the brokerage says. The stock is now 0.5% lower at 2,600.05 rupees. (santanu.choudhury@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0706 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korean exports are forecast to grow about 3% on-year for the whole of 2014, says a government source well informed of the country's external trade. Korea's annual trade volume will likely be slightly over $1.1 trillion dollars with exports expected to total $575 billion for this year, the source said. The expected 3% annual growth in exports falls short of an earlier estimate of over 6% but is still seen as "a good defense" against strong headwinds such as the weakening yen, a slowdown in Europe and sluggish global trade, says the source. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India won't oppose Mumbai High Court ruling on multinational tax. "India Won't Oppose Supreme Court Ruling on Multinational Tax -- Market Talk," at 0639 GMT, misstated in the headline that India's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Supreme Court\f28 \uc2 \f2  had ruled on a multinational tax issue. In fact it was the Mumbai High Court that had ruled on the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0639 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India is unlikely to challenge a court ruling that sided with British companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VOD.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Dutch Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RDSA.LN), saying they need not pay taxes on sales of shares with their Indian units. The move is likely to bring clarity among foreign companies operating in Asia's third largest economy, with regard to how their income is taxed in the country. Some of the other companies involved in similar cases include \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 GE\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  (T), and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mondelez International\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MDLZ) among others. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; Twitter: @jaikrishna) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0639 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India is unlikely to challenge a court ruling that sided with British companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VOD.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Dutch Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RDSA.LN), saying they need not pay taxes on sales of shares with their Indian units. The move is likely to bring clarity among foreign companies operating in Asia's third largest economy, with regard to how their income is taxed in the country. Some of the other companies involved in similar cases include \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 GE\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  (T), and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mondelez International\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MDLZ) among others. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; Twitter: @jaikrishna) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrections and Amplifications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This item was corrected at 06:57 GMT. The original misstated in the headline that India's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Supreme Court\f28 \uc2 \f2  had ruled on a multinational tax issue. In fact it was the Mumbai High Court that had ruled on the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0631 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Third-quarter gross domestic product data are still a day away, but Nomura analysts have already declared that India is on the verge of several years of economic strength--a "Goldilocks era" of fast growth and stable prices. This year was an "inflection year" for the country, Nomura says. Growth bottomed out, inflation eased and a new federal government provided political stability. But as the government of Prime Minister Narendra Modi works to promote investment, upgrade infrastructure and remove regulatory impediments to doing business, gradual recovery is all but inevitable, Nomura says. That includes India's long-ailing industrial sector: Already under Modi, electricity generation has jumped, cement consumption has grown and big investment projects are moving ahead. Other pieces are also moving into place: Firms are hiring more, corporations are starting to cut debt and rural wage growth--a big driver of food inflation--is coming down. The risks, Nomura says, "are more global than local." (raymond.zhong@wsj.com; Twitter: @zhonggg) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0627 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Based on the composition of its new basic portfolio and its second-quarter results, Japan's 120 trillion yen Government Pension Investment Fund (GPIF) still needs to shed domestic bonds by some 20.3 trillion yen, and increase its purchase of domestic stocks by 8.0 trillion yen, international bonds by 3.2 trillion yen and international stocks by 9.1 trillion yen, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Assuming the GPIF reduces domestic bonds at a similar pace with July-September, it may achieve its target weighting in 18 months, says house analyst Ryoko Tada. "Looking at supply and demand factors alone, stock prices could move higher, yen bond rates could rise and the yen could weaken further." Assuming the GPIF continues to shed domestic bonds at a pace similar to July-September, it would achieve its target weighting for domestic bonds by end-financial year 2015--while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's second round of quantitative easing is still in place (which calls for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise its bond purchases, he adds. Thus the GPIF's portfolio shift should have only a limited impact on yen bonds. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0618 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India is the fastest growing smartphone market in Asia says \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Data Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a technology research firm. During the quarter that ended September, smartphone shipments to the South Asian country grew 80% on-year to 23.3 million units, it said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005930.SE) led the market with a 24% share, followed by home-grown companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Micromax Informatics\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Lava International, which have 20% and 8% market share, IDC data show. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; Twitter: @jaikrishna) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0611 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Capital Economics revised down to 5.8% from 6.3% its 2014 growth forecast of the Philippines after third-quarter growth came in at 5.3%, way below consensus on weaker government spending. "However, with most of the factors that have underpinned strong growth in recent years still in place, we are keeping our forecast for 2015 at 6.5%," said Gareth Leather, Asia economist at Capital Economics. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0603 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] In a big relief to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VOD.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Dutch Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RDSA.LN), India's new government has decided not to challenge high court rulings in million dollar tax cases against the two multinationals in separate transfer pricing cases, says a senior finance ministry official. India is in litigation with a number of foreign firms for alleged tax evasion, which has been a obstacle for increased foreign investment in the country for the past few years. India's new government led by Narendra Modi seems to keeping its promise to end "tax terrorism" by making it easier for companies to do business in the country. (prasanta.sahu@wsj.com; Twitter: @pksahu9) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0551 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thai Finance Minister says the government is likely to raise the country's consumer tax or value-added tax (VAT) in a gradual manner from 7% to 10%. Hiking it to 10% in one go has raised concerns that Thailand could fall into a recession, as seen in Japan with its own sales tax hike last spring. Some economists believe that the VAT increase is going to be delayed due to Thailand's weak economy. "With slow economic recovery, we believe the junta government would delay the VAT hike, and [leave] it for the elected government to make a decision," says the Bangkok-based Tisco Economic Strategic Unit. "Ten percent VAT is very unlikely. The most possible hike if any is from 7% to 8%." (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com; Twitter: @waanspeaking) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 27, 2014 02:24 ET (07:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141127eabr000sf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
