\par\par \b \uc2 newsbox.ch/ Growth-friendly signals from Europe and Asia - Market comment by LGT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2014\par 10:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Growth-friendly signals from Europe and Asia \par  \par Over the past few days, we have had three economic policy events that should \par further underpin the global equity bull run over the medium term. In Europe, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  effectively brought the option of expanding the ECB's asset \par purchases to government bonds within reach. China surprised with its first cut \par in interest rates in more than two years, and Japan removed a short-term \par obstacle to its recovery by postponing a controversial consumption tax increase. \par  \par Please find below the market comment by Mikio Kumada, Global Strategist from LGT \par Capital Partners: \par  \par - Market comment (PDF) \par  \par - Photo Mikio Kumada (JPG) \par  \par For more information please contact: \par  \par Roland Cecchetto oder Kim Ghilardi \par  \par Communicators \par +41 44 455 56 66 \par  \par roland.cecchetto@communicators.ch \par  \par kim.ghilardi@communicators.ch \par  \par =------------------------------------------------------ \par  \par Growth-friendly signals from Europe and Asia \par  \par Over the past few days, we have had three economic policy events that should \par further underpin the global equity bull run over the medium term. In Europe, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  effectively brought the option of expanding the ECB's asset \par purchases to government bonds within reach. China surprised with its first cut \par in interest rates in more than two years, and Japan removed a short-term \par obstacle to its recovery by postponing a controversial consumption tax increase. \par  \par The equity bull market is alive and well. In the US and Japan, stocks have again \par hit new highs. In the deflation-plagued Eurozone, benchmark stock indices have \par now recouped about three-quarters of the steep losses they had recorded in the \par June-October selloff and Chinese market performance has improved as well. \par Riskier markets segments - ranging from Greek shares to US small caps - have \par also been performing well in recent weeks. Under the premise of an intact bull \par market, a robust revival of risk appetite after this summer's sharp correction \par would only be a logical "technical" development. But the rally is justifiable \par also from a fundamental view-point. Corporate earnings, as we have noted \par recently, are again generally exceeding expectations in most markets, and the \par past few days have brought some additional macroeconomic policy developments \par that should further underpin the bullish case. \par  \par 1) Envisioned government bond purchases by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par Speaking publicly on Friday, ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  stressed that the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would act to raise inflation expectations in the euro area "as soon \par as possible", adding that it was prepared to further expand its \par recently-launched asset purchase program. He did explicitly not rule out what is \par generally considered the most effective means to stoke inflation expectations: \par the buying of government bonds with freshly-printed money. Despite continued \par controversies about the legality of such purchases in Europe, the moment in \par which the ECB finally joins ranks with all other major central banks in his \par regard appeared to be coming markedly closer. \par  \par 2) General monetary policy easing in China \par On the same day, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  unexpectedly cut its one-year \par benchmark interest rates on loans and deposits from 6% to 5.6% and from 3% to \par 2.75%, respectively. This move, representing the first rate cuts since July \par 2012, suggests Beijing may be shifting gears in terms of monetary policy. \par Despite a significant economic slowdown in recent years, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has thus far \par refrained from a general monetary easing, pursuing a policy of reactive and \par selective liquidity injections instead. Thus, the latest cuts indicate that \par Beijing is now more willing to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to prevent any additional \par economic slowdown. \par  \par 3) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  removes controversial obstacle to growth \par In Japan, barely two weeks after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  surprised investors by \par massively expanding its "quantitative easing" program, events continued to \par unfold with exceptional speed. The preliminary data for the country's gross \par domestic product in the third quarter of 2014, published on 17 November, \par appeared to be so disappointingly weak that Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  promptly \par decided to turn the table on his critics: he postponed the long-planned but \par controversial second increase in the country's consumption tax until April 2017, \par and called for snap elections for 14 December. Thus, according to most \par economists, Japan removed an unnecessary short-term obstacle to growth, \par maximizing the potential impact of its aggressive monetary expansion. At the \par same time, the snap poll should further strengthen the reflationists from a \par domestic political viewpoint, ideally ensuring that Tokyo stays the course for \par another four years. \par  \par Bullish, but not without pitfalls \par Of course, there are risks to how these events will play out: the political and \par legal realities of Europe could still kill the hope of ECB government bonds \par purchases - or at least dilute the idea to a degree that would undermine the \par inflationary message. China's easing, even if it continues, could prove largely \par ineffective, given that the country's real problem is that it has too much, \par rather than too little, credit growth. Lastly, in Japan, no one can guarantee \par that Prime Minister Abe's all-or-nothing gamble will not backfire at the ballot \par box. Still, the bottom line is that the three largest economies after the United \par States are increasingly moving towards providing more economic stimuli, which \par should - sooner or later - be welcomed by the markets. \par  \par Monetary policy in domestic economic perspective \par A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increases/decreases the monetary base (and the potential total \par amount of money in circulation) by temporarily or permanently buying/selling \par securities in the capital markets. When it buys, it "prints" new money, and \par vice-versa. Normally, this activity can be visualized with the level of policy \par interest rates. But when these rates are close to zero, looking at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  balance sheet size is more helpful. In the first chart (see PDF, page 2), \par we show the balance sheets of major central banks relative to their territory's \par gross domestic product - i.e. we look at policy from a "national" viewpoint. The \par most extreme boost took place in Switzerland from 2008 to 2012. The changes in \par the US, China and the Eurozone look modest by comparison. In 2012, Japan took \par the lead as the most "aggressive" monetary activist, which explains the strong \par subsequent reactions of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock market. \par  \par Monetary policy in historical cross-comparison \par Next, we show how much the balance sheets have grown since end-2001- that is, we \par compare the absolute rates of change (see PDF, page 2). From this perspective, \par China has consistently pursued the most aggressive (and steady) balance sheet \par expansion, followed closely by the US since 2008. However, the US path has been \par more volatile, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  adjusted and temporarily suspended its \par "quantitative easing" program over the years. In both cases, the balance sheets \par have increased roughly sevenfold since end-2001. By contrast, from this \par viewpoint, Japan's expansion is modest, and started only after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par took over as the helm at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in March 2013. In that sense, Japan \par has plenty of upside room. The same is theoretically true for the Eurozone, \par where the balance sheet has been shrinking until recently. Following the most \par recent policy decisions, however, both balance sheets are set to either start \par increasing again (ECB), or accelerate their rate of growth (BOJ). China's recent \par interest rate cuts, meanwhile, should at the very least ensure that the \par country's monetary policy will remain on its stable uptrend. Taken together, \par these developments should more than offset the gradual slowing of the balance \par sheet expansion in the US, where the economy is performing quite well. \par  \par +-------------++--------------++----------------+ \par |Provider     ||Channel       ||Contact         | \par +-------------++--------------++----------------+ \par |Tensid Ltd., ||newsbox.ch    ||Provider/Channel| \par |Switzerland  ||\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.newsbox.ch||related"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.newsbox.ch||related}}}\uc2 \f2          | \par |\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tensid.ch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tensid.ch}}}\uc2 \f2 ||            