\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Japan Attempts to Put Brakes on Yen's Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitsuru Obe \par \uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 12:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Equities Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Japan's finance minister moved Friday for the first time to get the yen's rapid fall under control, the clearest sign yet that a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is beginning to complicate the country's aggressive experiment to restore growth.\par \par \uc2 In a break from his practice of not commenting on exchange rates, Taro Aso told a news conference the speed of the yen's recent decline was "too fast." He added, "There is no doubt about that."\par \par \uc2 The yen quickly jumped on his comments. But later in the day it lost much of its gains, suggesting verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have difficult overcoming the fundamental reason the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gotten so weak.\par \par \uc2 That reason is the divergence between U.S. and Japanese monetary policy. The Federal Reserve is looking to tighten next year, while the Bank of Japan on Oct. 31 announced new steps to flood the financial system with yen, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a downward slide. At one point Thursday, a dollar was changing hands at around Yen119, the weakest level for the yen in more than seven years and a 9% fall in just three weeks.\par \par \uc2 After Friday's gyrations, the dollar stood at around Yen117.80 in late Asia trading.\par \par \uc2 The yen's weakness has become a major topic of global debate, raising tensions between Japan and its major trading partners. Asian companies and government officials fear that Japanese exporters could grab market share through the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which allows them to lower prices and inflates their earnings because the dollars they bring in are worth more in yen terms.\par \par \uc2 Fed staffers have cut their medium-term U.S. growth projections partly on a rising dollar, according to minutes of the Fed's Oct. 28-29 meeting.\par \par \uc2 In Japan too, the weak yen is divisive. Prime Minister Shinzo Abe's policy of reviving inflation--largely carried out through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing--has played a key role in driving down the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Mr. Abe formally dissolved parliament, clearing the way for elections on Dec. 14. At a news conference Friday evening, the prime minister started out by defending his move to reverse the yen's strength under previous governments.\par \par \uc2 "No matter how much sweat they put in, no matter how good their ideas, [businesses] couldn't compete due to the strong yen, and many jobs were lost," he said.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have yet to increase the quantity of exports much, since many Japanese manufacturers moved overseas years ago, and the cost of imports such as food has risen. That has dealt a further blow to consumers already suffering from a national sales tax increase in April, posing an election risk for Mr. Abe. Opposition parties say Mr. Abe's economic policies have lowered the nation's standard of living.\par \par \uc2 Even the strongest supporters of Abenomics say they don't want the yen to get much weaker than Yen120 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe levels around Yen120 are acceptable, but if it weakens as much as to Yen125, that would make me a bit nervous, make me stop and think, "Is this OK?'" said Etsuro Honda, an adviser to Mr. Abe, in a recent interview.\par \par \uc2 Separately, a person close to the BOJ said one internal estimate suggested the dollar at Yen120 was "optimal." Adjusted for inflation and the weight of Japanese trade, the yen's rate is now the lowest since late 1982, according to BOJ data.\par \par \uc2 That background helps explain the Friday comments by Mr. Aso, but some economists say Japan might have to do more than just talk if it wants to reverse the trend. The dollar will likely rise to Yen120 by March next year, "driven by the broad current of dollar strength fueled by expectations that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates," Barclays wrote in a report to clients a few hours after Mr. Aso spoke.\par \par \uc2 Capital Economics, a London-based research firm, forecast that the dollar would buy Yen130 next year and Yen140 in 2016.\par \par \uc2 If Mr. Abe were serious about stemming the yen's fall, he would have to either abandon his 2% inflation target and make the BOJ scale back its easing measures, or "shoot a real bullet" by making the finance ministry directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and sell dollars to buy yen, said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Those steps would amount to Mr. Abe rejecting his own policies, said Mr. Minami. He characterized Mr. Aso's remarks as a goodwill gesture to voters and overseas governments, not a real policy shift.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aso himself suggested direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't on the table. A different government official added that the yen's current levels may be too low for some consumers but are beneficial for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economy as a whole, making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "out of the question."\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com and Mitsuru Obe at mitsuru.obe@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAE1120141121eabl0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal60