\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan finmin Aso: recent yen weakening 'too rapid'\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 03:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Verbal warning prompts a spike in the yen from 7-yr low\par \par \uc2 * Aso rules out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sticks to market-determined \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Negative effects of weak yen may become election issue (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Japan's finance minister warned on Friday about the yen's weakening, describing it as "too rapid", but he stuck to the government's stance of allowing markets to determine exchange rates and dismissed the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the slide.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters after a cabinet meeting, Taro Aso said rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, whether up or down, were undesirable.\par \par \uc2 Aso's remarks prompted a spike in the yen versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to as low as 117.45 yen from around 118 yen, after hitting a seven-year peak of 118.96 versus the yen earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's depreciation against the dollar over the past week has been too rapid ... A rapid swing in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whether up or down, should not be welcome as it causes various impacts," Aso said.\par \par \uc2 Aso dismissed the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be determined by the market, not by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 Aso expressed concern that the yen's excess depreciation would boost import costs of energy and other materials, when asked how the impact of a weak yen may become an issue in the upcoming general election expected on Dec. 14.\par \par \uc2 Aso's latest remarks stand in contrast with his previous stance that a weak yen was still a positive because it helps exporters' earnings.\par \par \uc2 Many small firms and households are worried that the rise in import costs is wiping out the benefits of increased profits.\par \par \uc2 The yen has tumbled around 10 yen since the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  stunned investors by expanding its quantitative easing programme on Oct. 31, while the dollar has firmed on expectations the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin raising interest rates next year.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  has reiterated that a weak yen is a positive for the Japanese economy on the whole.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in November 2011, to stem a strengthening yen. It last bought the yen to arrest its declline versus dollar in the late 1990s, when the country was grappling with the Asia financial crisis and its own banking crisis. (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by \uc2 Chris Gallagher\uc2  & Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-21T03:57:21.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141121eabl00238\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
