\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: GOP's Midterm Win In Focus, But Global Economy Bigger Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 1,550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2014\par 13:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Chatter in markets will focus on the resounding victory for the GOP in Tuesday's midterm elections, but for now it's hard to see how it would significantly affect investors. If anything it will be seen as opening the door to legislative changes that could be economically beneficial, so the current upbeat mood in U.S. markets could be bolstered further. More troubling is the continued bad news from economic data overseas. Earlier reports showed weak business activity in the services sectors of China and India, the two biggest emerging-market economies in the world, and a troubling decline in eurozone retail sales. Meanwhile, Bank of Japan Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  defended his institution's expanded asset-purchase program and made it clear he would do more in the future if inflation data continue to miss the BOJ's target. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S. Midterm elections results in a sweep for the Republicans, which regained a majority in \uc2 the Senate\uc2  and strengthened its hold on \uc2 the House\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The result will inevitably bolster the GOP's confidence and it may force President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , now isolated as a Democrat in the White House, into a more compromising posture on various issues. So, while partisan rancor is hardly likely to subside and the president is not going to give up his veto power easily, optimists in the investor community will see this as at least hopeful for projects such as a tax code overhaul. The Republicans may take a shot at repealing Obamacare, but without the 60-seat threshold required to prevent Democratic filibusters in \uc2 the Senate\uc2  that's unlikely to succeed. The result also is significant for the preparation for the 2016 presidential election, which effectively gets underway now. The GOP will read this as bolstering its chances of returning to the White House, with the midterm results interpreted as a resoundingly negative referendum on Mr. Obama's leadership. But the electorate is not happy with Republicans, either. And there could be reluctance to grant full power to one party. What's more, the GOP are yet to show that they can put up a candidates that's capable of both covering the demands of its own fractious membership, including its influential conservative win, and winning over the broader electorate. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: Markit Economics \uc2 HSBC\uc2  services purchasing managers index slipped to 52.9 in October from 53.5 in September.\par \par \uc2 This completes a rather gloomy set of Chinese PMIs, with the official services PMI on Monday as well as the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and official manufacturing PMIs all weakening and only hanging tentatively to the expansion signified by a plus-50 reading. The pressure for either or both the federal government and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to enact some kind of monetary and/or fiscal stimulus is now especially strong. Following a slippage in the yen in the wake of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's surprise move to add more stimulus, there also will be pressure on the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weaken the yuan. (MC)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 : In his first public speech since the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  announced a surprise move to increase its asset purchases, Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  strongly defended the move as one in which the BOJ was compelled by its commitment to a 2% inflation target. And he made it clear that more was possible if that data continue to show that the target is being elusive. "In order to completely overcome the chronic disease of deflation, medicine should be taken until the end," the governor said. "Half-baked medical treatment will only worsen the symptoms."\par \par \uc2 The prospect and the firm, unwavering commitment that Mr. Kuroda presented, will be taken as a signal to sell the yen. And for now, it also is being celebrated by equity investors who have continued to bid up Japanese stocks since Friday. Criticisms are already arising, however, mostly from the perspective that BOJ pump-priming will take pressure off the government to push through harder-to-sell but still-critical structural reforms to make the Japanese economy more competitive. (MC)\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE: September retail sales down 1.3% on the month against expectations of a 1.0% fall, and were up 0.6% on the year.\par \par \uc2 The latest eurozone retail sales data do nothing to alter the picture of a struggling economy, with weakness in both manufacturing and consumption. If there's one ray of hope it's that the region's banks will start to loosen credit supply now that the stress tests are over and with the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offering ultra-cheap funding. (AM)\par \par \uc2 EUROPE: October services purchasing managers' indexes.\par \par \uc2 -- EURZONE was 55.9 against 52.4 forecast and vs. 52.4 in September.\par \par \uc2 -- FRANCE was 48.3 against 48.1 forecast and vs. 48.4 in September.\par \par \uc2 -- GERMANY was 54.4 against 54.8 forecast and vs. 55.7 in September.\par \par \uc2 -- ITALY was 50.8 against 59.5 forecast and vs. 48.8 in September.\par \par \uc2 -- SPAIN was 55.9 vs. 55.8 in September.\par \par \uc2 -- U.K. was 56.2 against 58.5 forecast and vs. 58.7 in September.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales data might have been gloomy in September, but domestic demand is likely to have been stable in October if the latest European services purchasing managers' indexes are anything to go by. Among the major eurozone economies, only France showed contraction, and even there it was less bad than expected. The U.K.'s fell short, putting downward pressure on sterling, though the detail of the survey suggested some building wage pressures. (AM)\par \par \uc2 INDIA: The Markit Economics services PMI dropped to 50.0 in October from 51.6 in September.]\par \par \uc2 This is an especially disappointing result. India's services industry is more important to its economy than the sector is for most other emerging markets. At 50, the indicator suggests there is no growth at all in sector. There had been some hope that the more pro-market policy framework posed by new Prime Minister Narendra Modi will foster more business confidence and bolster activity -- as had been tentatively seen in earlier PMI readings this year. The reality is it will take time for the government to first implement reforms and, second, for the economy to respond to them. Meanwhile, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is taking a very conservative approach, resisting calls to cut rates even as inflation has eased. Once the inflation risk is fully purged, though, rates can come down and Mr. Modi's reforms could kick in. In other words, despite this data, there are still reasons why investors are more upbeat on India at the moment. (MC)\par \par \uc2 ICELAND: \uc2 Iceland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts its key interest rate to 5.75% from 6%.\par \par \uc2 With inflation staying below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.5% target for nearly the whole of this year and, most recently, falling to 1.9%, the Icelandic \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed its key interest rate. That's quite a turnaround for an economy that only a few years ago was battling a crashing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggered by a banking crisis. (AM)\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 POLAND: Time N/A. \uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2  interest rate decision.\par \par \uc2 Polish consumer prices fell for the third straight month in September, according to data out in mid-October. That definitely leaves room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its benchmark rate, which is now at 2%. (AM)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 8:15 a.m. EST. \uc2 Automatic Data Processing\uc2  October national employment report. [Private payrolls expected +220,000 on-month vs. +213,000 in September.]\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADP\uc2  numbers have been capturing the decent state of job growth in the U.S. economy. And that has tended for once to align quite well with the trend in the official jobs report from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . For that reason, this report could be more readily seen as a harbinger of the more important announcement to come on Friday. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 8:30. a.m. EST. Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond President \uc2 Jeffrey Lacker\uc2  speaks at George Washington University Law Annual Fall Financial Regulatory Reform Symposium.\par \par \uc2 The topic for the conference is "Financial Stability after Dodd Frank: Have We Ended Too Big to Fail?" (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 10 a.m. EST. Institute for Supply Management October nonmanufacturing report on business. [PMI expected 58 vs. 58.6 in September.]\par \par \uc2 The September services PMI from the ISM was very strong and economists only expect that to moderate slightly. If so it will underscore the notion that the economy's solid momentum in the third quarter has continued into the fourth. (MC)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN: 6:50 p.m. EST. (8:50 a.m. Thursday, Tokyo) \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  October monetary policy meeting minutes\par \par \uc2 How did Gov. Kuroda convince his fellow board members to take the drastic and little expected step of increasing its asset purchases at last week's meeting. The minutes could shed some light on that. (MC) \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 05, 2014 07:36 ET (12:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141105eab500271\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
