\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Tokyo won't  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to defend yen - JBIC head Watanabe\b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2014\par 11:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 * Former \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat says recent yen moves natural\par \par \uc2 * Tokyo won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend yen unless moves are abrupt\par \par \uc2 * BOJ to stand pat for now-Watanabe, who is close to Kuroda (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Yoshifumi Takemoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend a certain yen level as long as its fall is gradual, said \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe\uc2 , a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat who now heads the state-operated lender \uc2 Japan Bank for International Cooperation\uc2  (JBIC).\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who is close to Bank of Japan Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep monetary policy steady even if inflation does not hit its 2 percent target early next year.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think Kuroda will pay much heed to calls for him to act," he told Reuters on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Shinzo Abe has sought to pull Japan out of deflation with bold fiscal and monetary stimulus that weakened the yen, boosting profits of export-reliant manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 But the yen's 8 percent fall over three months, which took it to a six-year low against the dollar, has drawn complaints from some lawmakers and business sectors for inflating the cost of fuel and raw material imports. Nearly half of Japanese firms polled by Reuters think the government should start defending the yen at this month's dollar high of 110.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who as former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tsar still yields influence on Japan's exchange-rate policy, said recent dollar/yen moves centering around 104-105 yen were natural and reflected the diverging monetary policies of U.S. and Japanese central banks.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes a possibility if the yen's moves are abrupt and warrant smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Watanabe said. "But authorities won't act to bring dollar/yen levels to a certain level," particularly if the moves are gradual, he added.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who has close contact with U.S. policymakers, also said Washington likely won't complain about dollar rises against the yen and the euro, unless Japanese or European authorities directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to accelerate the moves.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied in the three months to September on a view that higher U.S. interest rates down the road would attract funds from the euro zone and Japan, where rates are likely to stay low. It traded at 106.90 yen on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Growing signs of gloom in Japan's economy have led to expectations the BOJ may ease policy further. In deploying its massive stimulus last April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it aimed to achieve its 2 percent inflation target in roughly two years.\par \par \uc2 Consumer inflation is still barely above 1 percent and many analysts doubt the BOJ can hit its target for much of next year.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who worked under Kuroda when they were both finance ministry bureaucrats, said the BOJ governor will not ease policy just because inflation doesn't hit 2 percent early next year.\par \par \uc2 "Kuroda would probably say things are on track as long as inflation approaches 1.5 percent early next year," Watanabe said. "Besides, it's doubtful whether adding further stimulus will be effective."\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs for three years to 2007, still yields influence on Japanese economic policy with his broad network of policymakers in and outside of Japan. (Editing by \uc2 Chris Gallagher\uc2  and \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-25T09:56:55.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141022eaam00asx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
