\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  COLUMN- Time for for the dollar to punch higher vs the yen?\b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2014\par 11:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- Neal Kimberley is an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst for Reuters. The opinions expressed are his own --\par \par \uc2 By Neal Kimberley\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 7 (Reuters)- The dollar may run higher versus the Japanese yen on the back of strong U.S. jobs data and a tick higher in U.S. inflation, and amid signs Japan's investors may again be looking offshore in pursuit of yield.\par \par \uc2 The pace at which Japanese investors have purchased foreign bonds has increased in recent weeks, data from \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed on June 26.\par \par \uc2 Since the start of Japan's financial year in April, Japanese investors have bought 5.2 trillion yen ($50.98 billion) worth of foreign bonds.\par \par \uc2 Those investors are probably motivated by the fact that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is currently buying 7 trillion yen of Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs) a month, ensuring the yield on the benchmark 10-year JGB remains below 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , Governor of the Bank of Japan, confirming on Monday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to maintain its massive stimulus programme for as long as needed to meet its 2 percent inflation target, yields look set to stay very low.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, investors will be acutely aware that equivalent U.S. 10-year yield is 2.655 percent, and may be tempted once again to pursue better returns offshore, selling yen for dollars in the process.\par \par \uc2 For a graphic of JGB yields: ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/pup88t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/pup88t}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The fact that Japan's $1.24 trillion Government Pension Investment Fund (GPIF), the world's largest, is being pressed by Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's administration to seek higher returns will also not have gone unnoticed.\par \par \uc2 With GPIF's core weighting for foreign stocks and bonds currently at 12 and 11 percent respectively, there is clearly room for a slight increase in exposure, on top of the fund's professed intent to own fewer JGBs and more Japanese stocks.\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors will also understand the implications of Friday's appointment of Tatsuo Yamasaki as vice finance minister for international affairs at \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF).\par \par \uc2 Yamasaki served at the MOF as director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division in 2002-2004, a period in which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent about 35 trillion yen ($344 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem yen strength.\par \par \uc2 If nothing else his appointment may give food for thought to traders who might be contemplating renewed yen weakness.\par \par \uc2 JOBS AND PRICES\par \par \uc2 On the U.S. side of the equation, prices are ticking higher. Personal consumption expenditures (PCE) were up 1.8 percent in May, the largest gain since October 2012, and following a 1.6 percent rise in April.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, U.S. employment growth surged in June with the closely watched nonfarm payrolls data showing an increase of 288,000 jobs and the unemployment rate declining to near a six-year low of 6.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 For graphics on U.S. inflation and unemployment: ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/duq93t)(http://link.reuters.com/wam54t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/duq93t)(http://link.reuters.com/wam54t}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Investors generally, and Japanese investors in particular, might conclude such a combination of data ultimately leads to higher U.S. yields.\par \par \uc2 Others may think the U.S. Federal Reserve under Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  will choose to keep rates longer for lower in an attempt to erode slack in the jobs market and prompt a brisker rise in U.S. wages.\par \par \uc2 That would be likely to benefit U.S. equities, and the Dow Jones Industrial Average did indeed top 17,000 for the first time after Thursday's jobs data.\par \par \uc2 Investors pay their money and make their choice.\par \par \uc2 But either way, that choice may now involve buying the dollar versus the yen. (Editing by Nigel Stephenson and Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-7T10:12:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140707ea77005sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
