\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 01:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The dollar ticked lower against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday in the wake of Federal Reserve Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's testimony on Capitol Hill and the release of disappointing jobless-claims data.\par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.2% to $1.3711, while the dollar slipped 0.3% to 102.08 yen. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lost ground against the British pound, Swiss franc and the New Zealand dollar.The New Zealand dollar gained 0.7% against the greenback, to $0.8368, benefiting from positive data. New Zealand reported a record January trade surplus, stemming from robust Chinese demand for the country's dairy exports.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD--to trade in higher range after hitting two-week low 1.3643 Thursday. Spotlight on 1000 GMT February flash estimate euro area inflation. EUR/USD supported by surprise rise in euro-zone economic sentiment indicator to 101.2 in February--its highest level since July 2011--from in 101.0 January (versus forecast for drop to 100.5); weaker dollar sentiment; improved risk appetite; euro demand on rebounding EUR/GBP cross. But EUR/USD gains tempered by tensions over Ukraine; ECB data showing lending to the private sector in the euro zone declined by 2.2% in annual terms in January after a 2.3% drop in December; lower-than-expected Germany February preliminary CPI of +0.5% on month (versus forecast +0.6%); speculation that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might ease monetary policy at its meeting next week; positions adjustment before weekend. Other data: 0700 GMT Germany January retail sales; 0830 GMT January EuroCOIN indicator of euro area economic activity; 1000 GMT euro-zone January unemployment. Daily chart mixed as stochastics falling from overbought, positive MACD histogram bars contracting; but bullish hammer candlestick pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 1.3727 (Thursday's high), then at 1.3757 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 1.3768-1.3775 band (Tuesday's high-Jan. 2 high), then 1.3819 (Dec. 20 high), 1.3894 (Dec. 27 swing high) and psychological 1.4000 line. Support at 1.3643 (Thursday's low); breach would target 1.3631 (100-day moving average), then 1.3585 (Feb. 13 low), 1.3563 (Feb. 12 low) and 1.3551 (Feb. 7 low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY--to consolidate after hitting one-week low 101.72 Thursday. Underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid improved investor risk sentiment (VIX fear gauge eased 2.16% to 14.04; S&P rose 0.49% overnight) as Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  in her testimony before U.S. Senate Banking Committee said harsh weather has played a role in recent "soft data," although she isn't sure to what extent, but added that the Fed might consider a pause in its reduction of bond buying if the weakness persists; while U.S. durable goods orders fell smaller-than-expected 1.0% in January (versus forecast 2.0% drop). USD/JPY also supported by demand from Japan importers; expansionary \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's monetary policy. But risk appetite dented by persistent uncertainty about China's yuan policy; concerns about possible Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine. USD/JPY gains also tempered by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter sales; lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields; weaker dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 80.28 versus 80.43 early Thursday) on larger-than-expected 14,000 rise in U.S. jobless claims to 348,000 in week ended Feb. 22 (versus 335,000 forecast), fall in \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2 's manufacturing composite index to +4 in February from +5 in January; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 2330 GMT Japan January labor force survey, January Nation CPI, February Tokyo CPI, January household spending; 2350 GMT Japan January preliminary industrial production, January preliminary retail sales; 0500 GMT Japan January housing starts, January construction orders; 1330 GMT U.S. 4Q 2nd estimate GDP; 1445 GMT U.S. February ISM-Chicago PMI; 1455 GMT U.S. February \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  survey of consumers (final); 1500 GMT U.S. January pending home sales index. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode. Resistance at 102.45 (Thursday's high), then at 102.61-102.68 band (Wednesday's high-Monday's high); breach would target 102.83 (Feb. 21 high), then 102.94 (Jan. 31 high), 103.45 (Jan. 29 high) and 103.58 (Jan. 24 high). Support at 101.72 (Thursday's low); breach would target 101.67 (Feb. 20 low), then 101.38 (Feb. 17 low), 101.25 (Feb. 6 low) and 100.80-100.76 (Feb. 5 low-Feb. 4 low).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD--to trade in higher range. Supported by weaker dollar sentiment; improved risk appetite. But GBP/USD gains tempered by sterling sales on rebounding EUR/GBP cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0005 GMT U.K. February consumer confidence survey, 0700 GMT February Nationwide house price index, 0930 GMT January Land Registry house price index. Daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode, but stochastics neutral, five-day moving average meandering sideways. Resistance at 1.6697-1.6701 (Thursday's high-Wednesday's high), then at 1.6726 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 1.6733-1.6740 band (Feb. 19 high-Feb. 18 high), then 1.6822 (Feb. 17 four-year high), 1.6877 (Nov. 16, 2009, swing high) and psychological 1.7000 line. Support at 1.6615 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.6581 (Monday's low), then 1.6536 (50% Fibonacci correction of advance from Feb. 5 low of 1.6250 to Feb. 17 high of 1.6822), 1.6486 (55-day moving average), 1.6468 (61.8% Fibonacci correction) and 1.6424 (Feb. 12 low).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF--to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by weaker dollar sentiment; flows to haven CHF amid tensions in Ukraine; franc demand on rebounding CHF/JPY cross and on soft EUR/CHF cross. But CHF sentiment dented by weaker-than-expected Switzerland 4Q GDP growth of +1.7% on-year (versus forecast +2.1%). USD/CHF downside also limited by positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0800 GMT Switzerland February KOF economic barometer. Daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish at oversold, but MACD still in bearish mode, five-day moving average meandering sideways, inside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 0.8869-0.8862 band (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low), then at 0.8851 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8796 (Dec. 27 swing low), then 0.8758 (Nov. 3, 2011, low) and 0.8566 (Oct. 27, 2011, reaction low). Resistance at 0.8918 (Thursday's high), then at 0.8930-0.8940 band (Wednesday's high-Feb. 14 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9009 (Feb. 13 high), then 0.9038 (Feb. 12 high) and 0.9063 (Feb. 6 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD--to trade in lower range after hitting four-day high 1.1159 Thursday. Undermined by weaker dollar sentiment; improved risk appetite. But USD/CAD losses tempered by softer oil prices (Nymex crude settled 19 cents lower Thursday at $102.40/bbl); dovish \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's monetary policy stance; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 1330 GMT Canada December and 4Q GDP. Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, five-day moving average above 15-day MA and advancing. Support at 1.1069 (Wednesday's low), then at 1.1050 (Monday's low); breach would target 1.1011 (hourly chart), then 1.0906-1.0901 (Feb. 19 low-Jan. 16 low, near 55-day moving average). Resistance at 1.1159 (Thursday's high); breach would target 1.1179 (Feb. 21 high), then 1.1224-1.1233 band (Jan. 31 4 1/2-year high--50% Fibonacci retracement of 1.3063-0.9403 March 9, 2009-July 26, 2011, decline) and 1.1666 (61.8% retracement).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD--to trade in higher range after hitting three-week low 0.8902 Thursday. Supported by weaker dollar sentiment; improved risk appetite; month-end portfolio hedge adjustments. But AUD/USD gains tempered by concerns over economic slowdown in China, Australia's biggest trading partner; data showing Australia's fourth-quarter capital expenditure shrank 5.2% from the previous quarter; Aussie sales on soft AUD/NZD cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics bearish, but bullish hammer candlestick pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 0.8970 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9025 (Wednesday's high), then 0.9043-0.9049 band (Tuesday's high-Monday's high), 0.9079-0.9086 band (Feb. 18 high-Jan. 13 reaction high), 0.9102 (100-day moving average) and 0.9167 (Dec. 10 reaction high). Support at 0.8902 (Thursday's low, near 55-day moving average); breach would expose downside to 0.8870 (Feb. 5 low), then 0.8728 (Feb. 4 low).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting eight-day high 0.8391 Thursday. Supported by weaker dollar sentiment; improved risk appetite; Kiwi demand on soft AUD/NZD cross; hawkish \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 's monetary policy stance. But NZD/USD gains tempered by concerns over economic slowdown in China; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD in bullish mode, stochastics turning bullish; five- & 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 0.8391-0.8392 (Thursday's high-Feb. 17 high); breach would expose upside to 0.8432 (Jan. 14 reaction high), then 0.8543 (Oct. 22 swing high). Support at 0.8320 (hourly chart), then at 0.8289 (Thursday's low); breach would target 0.8277 (Wednesday's low), then 0.8258 (Monday's low), 0.8240 (Feb. 20 low), 0.8206 (Feb. 6 low) and 0.8184 (Feb. 5 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar late Thursday on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan's downside room was limited, however, as investors took profits after the dollar's recent strength. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.1284 around 0830 GMT compared with Wednesday's close of CNY6.1248. It traded in a range of CNY6.1210 to CNY6.1340. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.1224, higher than Wednesday's 6.1192. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts fell to 6.1475/6.1515 from 6.1495/6.1525 late Wednesday, implying a 0.3% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 28-02-14 0043GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140228ea2s0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
