\par\par \b \uc2 *South Korea Finance Minister Urges Japan Not to Rely on Yen Weakness\b0\par\par\uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2014\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 21 Feb 2014 04:19 EDT *South Korea Finance Minister: Skepticism Over Japan's 'Abenomics' Growing Globally\par \par \uc2 21 Feb 2014 04:19 EDT *South Korea Finance Minister Sees Effects of Yen Weakness Hurting Korea's Exports\par \par \uc2 21 Feb 2014 04:19 EDT *South Korea Finance Minister Urges Japan to Focus on Structural Reforms to Boost Economy\par \par \uc2 21 Feb 2014 04:19 EDT  South Korea Urges Japan Not to Rely on Yen Weakness\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By James Glynn \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SYDNEY--South Korea's finance minister urged Japan to push ahead with structural reforms rather than relying on yen weakness to spur its economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, Hyun Oh-seok said Friday that global skepticism over Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe's stimulus program, known as "Abenomics," has begun to grow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the sidelines of a gathering of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  finance officials in Sydney, Mr. Hyun said Japan's strong focus on yen depreciation would ultimately do little to lift the world's third-largest economy out of its long-term economic malaise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unless structural reform is conducted in the Japanese economy, my concern is that the recovery of the Japanese economy could be far from sustainable," Mr. Hyun said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's massive monetary and fiscal stimulus, begun last year, aims to boost the beleaguered economy by pushing down the yen, boosting exporters' profits and giving a jolt to Japanese stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's weakness has caused frictions in Asia and fears of beggar-thy-neighbor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluations. South Korea's won hit a five-year high against the yen in January, causing complaints from Seoul, which competes with Japan in foreign markets for goods ranging from automobiles to electronics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in recent years to slow sharp movements in the won. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  last month urged Seoul to let the market determine the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's level and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only to smooth fluctuations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But South Korean officials say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness is an issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I believe the depreciation of the yen will greatly affect the Asian economy in particular, if the trend continues in the future," Mr. Hyun said. "It's not imminent right now, but the negative impact will expand and further affect Korea's exports."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, Mr. Hyun urged Japan to make structural overhauls to boost its economy. Mr. Abe has promised to push ahead with liberalizing employment rules and opening up more to foreign competition. So far, though, these efforts have made less headway than the monetary and fiscal stimulus program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan should turn its eyes to aggressive opening of its market and the structural reforms that Korea has been choosing," Mr. Hyun said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G-20 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors are set to meet this weekend in Sydney. On Friday, officials held preparatory meetings. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  values are among a roster of contentious issues up for discussion. Developing countries also want assurances the U.S. won't back out too quickly from its extraordinary monetary policies, causing market turbulence across the globe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea has performed more strongly than many countries in the past nine months since the U.S. Federal Reserve began talking about winding up its massive monetary stimulus program. Unlike many Asian economies, the nation runs a hefty current-account surplus, making it less reliant on foreign capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's current-account surplus hit a fresh high of $70.73 billion in 2013, up from the previous record of $48.08 billion in the previous year, driven by solid exports. The country also has sizable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which stood at a record high of $348.39 billion as of end-January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't suffer any drastic losses during the latter half of 2013 when the Fed's anticipated rollback of stimulus triggered a repatriation of funds from emerging markets and a selloff of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The won, instead, benefited from strong inflows to South Korean markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Mr. Hyun said the yen's fall had contributed to a 10% drop in exports from South Korea to Japan over the past year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen last year fell more than 20% against the won. Bank of Korea Governor Kim Choong-soo said last month that the yen's slide over the past year had hurt several South Korean industries--steel, autos, machinery and electrical appliances--that compete directly with Japan in international markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Hyun's comments came as U.S. Treasury Secretary Jacob Lew said it was time for Japan to pursue the "third arrow" of its recovery program, the focus of which is promoting growth. The first two arrows were monetary easing and government spending.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They need to get on with the third arrow of Abenomics, which is reform, " he said Friday in Sydney.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy began to recover last year. But growth slowed in the fourth quarter, raising questions about whether the jolt from Abenomics will be sustained.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 David Rogers contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to James Glynn at james.glynn@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 21, 2014 04:19 ET (09:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140221ea2l001qe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
