\par\par \b \uc2 Investors brace for weaker yen against kiwi, real\b0\par\par\uc2 1,042 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2014\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * South Korea could act to slow won's rise against yen\par \par \uc2 * Not much upside seen for Aussie dollar-yen\par \par \uc2 * Brazilian real "uridashi" bonds could grease yen's slide\par \par \uc2 By Lisa Twaronite\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 17 (Reuters) - The yen has more room to fall against many widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the months ahead under the weight of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's ultra easy monetary policy, though its downward progress will be uneven as the policies of rival central banks diverge.\par \par \uc2 A clutch of rival \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have benefited from the dollar's surge against the yen over the past year, prompting trading partners such as China and South Korea to publicly express fears that their exports might be casualties of Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's "Abenomics" policies aimed at sparking growth and vanquishing persistent deflation.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could bump to new multi-year lows in 2014 against New Zealand's dollar and face downward pressure against Brazil's real as those countries' central banks hike interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Its losses against the Australian dollar and South Korea's won may be more limited, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Sydney in no rush to tighten in the face of a sluggish economy and traders wary of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Seoul to cap its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) is committed to maintaining its ultra-loose policy as it aims for a target of 2 percent inflation within 2 years, while seeking to cushion any blow to the economy from a hike in the country's sale tax in April.\par \par \uc2 Against this monetary backdrop, market participants say a weaker yen should catch no one off guard.\par \par \uc2 "Derivatives-wise, I do think people are positioned properly for a weaker yen, whether it would be in options or just a flat-out long dollar-yen or cross-yen position," said Bart Wakabayashi, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a lot of attention on dollar-yen, but if you look at the crosses, like euro-yen, sterling-yen, there are positions there, too."\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR SURGE\par \par \uc2 The dollar surged more than 21 percent against the yen in 2013, its biggest yearly percentage gain against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1979, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 The yen's weakness has been a consequence of the BOJ's massive burst of money-printing - almost $70 billion a month - under which it buys the equivalent of 70 percent of new Japanese government bond (JGB) issuance.\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. Federal Reserve starting to taper its own monetary stimulus, yield differentials will likely continue to favour the dollar - and by extension other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or "crosses" - over the yen.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely face some scattered rebounds on profit-taking, as it did earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 And a contrarian view argues the yen could gain in 2014, as the tax hike drags on growth and the BOJ exhausts its asset-purchasing arsenal, pushing up JGB yields and stemming fund outflows from Japanese investors seeking higher yields abroad.\par \par \uc2 Still, a solid majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists in Reuters' latest monthly poll said the yen would be among the worst-performing developed world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first six months of 2014, with the consensus calling for the yen to trade at 104 per dollar in one month, 106 in six and 110 in a year.\par \par \uc2 Speculators' net yen short positions - bets against the yen - stood at 128,868 contracts as of Jan. 7, edging down for the second straight week but still close to a 6-year high.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of this yen-short positioning is done by hedge funds who put these positions on, but there's a lot of scope for some more real money flows, with a lot of domestic outflows that still have to take place in Japan," said Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISK\par \par \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  forecasts the yen will fall to 8.87 South Korean won by the end of 2015, down from a 5-year low below 10 touched earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 "We still expect it [the won] to go higher, but there's a risk of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop that move in the won/yen cross, so I think markets are a bit wary," Kotecha said.\par \par \uc2 A slowdown in the yen's decline against the won would offer some relief to Korean car makers such as \uc2 Hyundai Motor Co\uc2  , who have been struggling to maintain competitive pricing against Japanese rivals.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar is unlikely to rally much against the yen in the face of a \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  campaign to "talk down" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The RBA is also seen keeping rates steady at a record low of 2.5 percent for now.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the Aussie/yen plunged more than 1 percent on Thursday to a near 1-month low of 92.13 yen, dragged down by the Aussie's skid to a more than 3-year low against the U.S. dollar after dismal Australian employment data revived speculation that interest rates could go even lower.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, however, is trading above 87 yen, close to a 6-year high, as the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  flashes hawkish signals.\par \par \uc2 That could help revive the kiwi's appeal for the so-called carry trade, a popular strategy before the global finiancial crisis in which investors borrowed low-yielding yen to buy assets in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The kiwi, as well as Brazil's real, could also get some help from foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, called uridashi, sold to yield-hungry Japanese retail investors.\par \par \uc2 Real-denominated gross uridashi issuance represented more than half of the total in November, and reached its highest level since July 2010, according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 Brazil surprised economists on Wednesday by raising its benchmark interest rate to 10.5 percent - a 2-year high- to tame of surging inflation. (Additional reporting by Dominic Lau and Hideyuki Sano in Tokyo, Ian Chua and John Noonan in Sydney, and Masayuki Kitano in Singapore; Editing by Alex Richardson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-YEN/ | ABN | M | E | RBN | D | J | AUF | ND | ABX | Z | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140116ea1g00157\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
