\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Stocks Strongest Weekly Resistance to Outflows in Emerging Asia -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2013\par 04:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore was the only country in emerging Asia to record positive flows into its equity market for the week to Nov. 13, according to a mutual fund survey by ANZ Research. Around US$18 million flowed into the city-state during the week, compared with outflows of $118 million from Indonesia and $127 million from Thailand. "The growth is not fantastic, but if you look at Southeast Asia [by comparison], Singapore is doing fairly well," says Irene Cheung at ANZ. Strong jobs data from the U.S. triggered outflows from the region in the last week as investors judged that U.S. stimulus spending may end sooner than previously thought, but outflows have stabilized on dovish comments Thursday from Janet Yellen, who is likely to be the next Federal Reserve chairwoman. Singapore shares are down 0.4% at 3199.4 month-to-date, the lowest decline in Southeast Asia. Malaysia's KLCI is down 1%, while Indonesia's JSX has lost 3.5%. (jake.watts@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] The local OIS curve is still tipping March as the most likely start of the RBNZ tightening cycle but BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones says "ongoing strength in the New Zealand dollar's trade-weighted index and a 'wait and see' approach on housing will stay the bank's hand until June." In that case, the "NZD would feel the weight of a falling NZ-U.S. interest rate differential," he says. The market may need to see weakness in upcoming housing data to be convinced, but while that may weigh "we suspect the actions of U.S. policy makers will ensure that NZD/USD support levels continue to hold," he adds. Jones says another push to the topside is the biggest risk in coming weeks; he is sticking with his 0.8400 year-end forecast. The pair is at 0.8292. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] Stock markets are up in Asia buoyed by highs on Wall Street overnight but \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't in sync. Equity bulls are back in force following reassurance from incoming Federal Reserve governor Janet Yellen that bond buying won't end prematurely. Japan's Nikkei and Seoul's Kospi index are both up 1.7% while Shanghai's Composite index is up 1.4% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng has gained 1.2%. But contrary to the typical correlation between domestic stocks and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are struggling. The likely reason, according to a trader, is the proxy effect the weakening Japanese yen has on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen fell sharply on Thursday on hints from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister that the authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap excessive yen strength. It is now well below the Y100-a-dollar barrier. The weakness in Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit Thursday just moments after the yen started to fall. Many Asia central banks actively manage the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by measuring their relative values against an index of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of trading partners--which usually includes Japan. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  (8306.TO) and \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group (8411.TO) \uc2 rise after the banks revised up their full-year earnings forecast for the FY2013. Mitsubishi UFJ FG is up 1.2% at Y653 while Mizuho FG is up 1.9% at Y218. Mitsubishi UFJ revised up its full-year net profit estimate to Y910 billion, exceeding Quick analysts consensus of Y833.2 billion, from an earlier estimate of Y760 billion, on the back of lower credit cost forecast and improvements in net gains on equity holdings. Meanwhile, Mizuho FG raised its full-year net profit forecast to Y600, also above Quick analysts consensus of Y568.7 billion, from Y500 billion previously, on the back of a strong performance at its fee business and solid profits at its brokerage firm. Although the full-year upward revisions are positive, Mitsubishi UFG FG and Mizuho FG need to improve shareholder returns to deliver sustainable share-price gains, says Shin Tamura, analyst at Barclays Securities Japan. (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] BMD CPO futures could extend declines on Friday, as weak technical signals may attract follow-through selling, a trading executive in Kuala Lumpur says, adding that the MYR2,550/ton remains important support and declines under that level would suggest further downside in the coming sessions. Benchmark January CPO ended down 0.6% at MYR2,588/ton Wednesday; CBOT January soyoil is up 0.1% at 41.29 cents/lb in screen trade. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan is trading flat in spite of a lower guidance for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a lack of fresh, market-moving economic news, traders say. The dollar-yuan is around 6.0915 from its Thursday close of 6.0922. Earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the pair at 6.1351 from 6.1315 on Monday. The WSJ Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 73.389 late Thursday in New York from 73.274 late on Wednesday. "The dollar market isn't moving much recently so the yuan isn't either," a trader with a local bank says. "The yuan could be in consolidation mode until the end of the year given that the fixings have been relatively stable in recent sessions," he adds. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts are up at 6.2690/6.2720 from 6.1590/6.1615 late on Thursday. (chao.deng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The won could be hampered by weakness in the Japanese yen which slumped Thursday on comments from Japan's finance minister suggesting the government wants to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak. The South Korea won is most particularly affected by yen weakness in Asia as exporters of both countries compete in many areas, which has led to \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  keeping a close eye on the yen/won cross exchange rate, sometimes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to ensure price stability. USD/KRW could therefore be supported as long as USD/JPY stays above 100.00, despite dollar-bearish remarks by incoming U.S. Federal Reserve governor Janet Yellen. Ms. Yellen's comments in her testimony Thursday practically removed the fear of an early end to the Federal Reserve's loose monetary policy. USD/KRW is now 1,067.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] The initial target for the USD/JPY is a technical chart point at 100.60 vs. last 100.24, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo, but adds that it is a small target. Usual USD/JPY-sellers such as Japanese exporters, are in a wait-and-see mood as they seem to bet the current USD/JPY upward momentum could be long-lasting, possibly reaching 103 or higher in weeks to come, he says. In addition to robust Japanese share prices, expectations for Japan's GPIF reform proposals due this month, rising expectations for the BOJ's additional easing next year after Japan's slower growth announced Thursday, as well as Fed's Yellen signaling that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its policy easy for time being...all favor the USD/JPY, he says.\par \par \uc2 (takashi.mochizuki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] The ringgit currently at 3.1960 to the dollar is slightly stronger than its Thursday closing of 3.2030, as the market awaits release of Malaysia's 3Q Gross Domestic Product data at 1000 GMT. The median forecast is for GDP to have risen 4.8% versus a gain of 4.3% in the last quarter. But in the meantime, the ringgit may find it hard to rally further as the U.S. dollar retains a bullish bias inside the Bollinger uptrend channel that supports USD/MYR at 3.1940. The ringgit could look vulnerable next week if USD/MYR ends Friday above the weekly Bollinger mid resistance at 3.2180. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold slips slightly in early Asia, but dovish comments from Janet Yellen, the nominee to lead the U.S. Federal Reserve, could help provide a floor to prices. The yellow metal had pushed higher overnight following the release of prepared remarks from Yellen, who said the job market and economy were running short of where they could be and said that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had "more work to do" to aid the economic recovery, points she emphasized in testimony to the Congress on Thursday. "She's reaffirming that the economy needs stimulus," says Robin Bhar, a metals analyst with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "I don't think anything is new there, but (the remarks) are confirmation and comfort for the gold market," he says. Some investors buy gold as a hedge against the inflation that can follow \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to inject liquidity in the economy. Spot gold is at $1,286.95/oz, down 35 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals are likely to be confined to ranges as market weighs the positives about the U.S. Federal Reserve continuing with its monthly bond buying program, while worries about economic growth in China may weigh on sentiment. Answering questions during her confirmation hearing to lead the Federal Reserve, Janet Yellen said that the timing for the Fed to pull back its $85-billion monthly bond-buying program hinges on the health of the economy. Analysts say Yellen's comments provided some support to the market overnight. For metal prices, "it is all about the U.S. Fed at the moment," says Myrto Sokou, an analyst at Sucden Financial. LME 3-month copper finished up 0.2% at $6,992/ton Thursday, while aluminum closed 0.2% lower at $1,791/ton. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is flat at 5353.1, failing to match expectations of a rise following offshore gains, as resources and banks limp into the weekend. "The Aussie dollar is weak and it feels like, with the volume so low, we may be seeing a little bit of offshore selling," says Market Matters editor Shawn Hickman. "There was no significant buying in BHP overnight and resources have had a good run." BHP (BHP.AU), \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) and Fortescue (FMG.AU) are down 0.5%-0.6%, and CBA (CBA.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) are down 0.3%. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 14, 2013 22:53 ET (03:53 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131115e9bf000k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
