\par\par \b \uc2 RUBBER-Tokyo futures rise on Yellen comments, on track for weekly gain\b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2013\par 02:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 15 (Reuters) - Benchmark TOCOM rubber futures rose on Friday as the prospect of extended U.S. monetary stimulus boosted the outlook for demand, with the contract on course for its biggest weekly gain in a month.\par \par \uc2 FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 * The Tokyo Commodity Exchange rubber contract for April delivery <0#2JRU:> was up 2.8 yen at 261.9 yen per kg by 0042 GMT, after settling 1.3 yen higher on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 * Friday's gains put the contract on track for a weekly gain of 1.9 percent, its highest percentage change since the week to Oct 11.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , likely to be the next Federal Reserve chief, on Thursday robustly defended the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bold steps to spur economic growth.\par \par \uc2 * For the top stories in rubber market and other news, click , or\par \par \uc2 MARKET NEWS\par \par \uc2 * The U.S. dollar was quoted around 100.16 yen in Asia, its highest level in over two months, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister said the country must retain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy tool.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark Nikkei stock average rose 1.1 percent in early Friday trade as the weaker yen lifted support for exporters, the index's main component.\par \par \uc2 * The 19-commodity Thomson Reuters/CoreCommodity CRB index settled 0.1 percent higher on Thursday, supported by stronger oil and metals prices.\par \par \uc2 DATA/EVENTS (GMT)\par \par \uc2 * The following data is expected on Friday: (Time in GMT)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1330  U.S.   New York Fed Empire State survey\par 1