\par\par \b \uc2 Commerz' EUR/USD Order Book Has Small Upside Bias -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2013\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's EUR/USD Order Book Model shows a small upside bias Thursday. It had more buy than sell orders when the snapshot was taken at 0700 GMT and the spot traded at 1.3457. However, at levels around 1.3480, buy and sell densities become about equal, suggesting that an upmove would struggle here. EUR/USD now at 1.3467.(gary.stride@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] The recovery from the 125-265 retracement level and the subsequent move through 126-18 activated a new December \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  sell signal noted \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Says that "the MACD remains below its zero line reflecting a bearish trend is in force and that the risk remains for a resumption of downside and break below the 125-265 support. This will trigger a more prolonged sell-off below the 125-165 October low and onto the deeper 62% retracement at 124.145, and even the 122-07 September low." The December UST contract trades at 126-200. nick.cawley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/JPY breaks firmly above 160.00 for the first time in 4-years just as Tokyo passes the baton to Europe. That move triggers JPY slippage elsewhere. USD/JPY rises to a fresh 2-month high of 99.81, EUR/JPY a fresh November high of 134.43. GBP/JPY trades at 160.18, highest since August 2009. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD nudges lower, largely on the back of Fed president nominee Janet Yellen's prepared dovish remarks, two traders say. The pair is at 29.606 versus 29.650--the highest onshore closing level in over six weeks--late Wednesday in Taipei. They say the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying the greenback around 29.56 to keep the trading band narrow. But they add that continued outflows from local stocks (foreign investors sold NT$5.93 billion worth of Taiwan shares Thursday), exporter selling and Taiwan's benign economic growth profile are set to keep the USD/TWD in a tight range with a downward bias. One of them tips a band of 29.50-29.60 for Friday. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swedish unemployment rate is expected to slip to 7.4% in October from 7.5% in the previous month according to the DJN survey forecast. However, SEB says, despite some signs of a stronger labour market it believes unemployment is slightly higher than the Riksbank forecasts, and so looks for an unchanged 7.5% rate for October. EUR/SEK now trades at 8.98, resistance comes in at 9.00 and 9.0455 while support sits at 8.90. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds are little changed after earlier data showed the country's economy grew 0.3% q/q, in line with consensus, but lower than the 0.7% q/q reading recorded in Q2. Euro zone GDP growth for Q3 will be released at 1000 GMT. The December Bund is 0.10 higher at 141.43 and the 10-year cash yields 1.723%. nick.cawley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were at the mercy of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  speak overnight. Late in Europe Wednesday the ECB's Praet brought up the subject of negative deposit rates again and sent EUR/USD tumbling below 1.34. Incoming Fed chairman Yellen repaired some of that damage and generally weighed on the USD with comments that suggested Fed tapering was still a way off. Then in Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Fin Min Aso hinted at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to curb one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. No surprisingly, Yellen's comments saw US yields fall and give a lift to equity futures, this after the Dow and S&P had both closed at record highs, the Nikkei followed up with a 2% gain and a 6-month high. For Thursday, the main focus is the US Senate hearing on Yellen's nomination (1500 GMT), beforehand UK retail sales and Euro zone GDP grab the spotlight. Already out, German 3Q GDP met consensus at +0.3%, a sharp slowdown from the 2Q +0.7% print, while France fell back into contraction with a -0.1% 3Q print against +0.1% expected. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR climbs back above the trading barrier of 3.2000 as the initial dollar-selling momentum attributed to dovish Fed-speak has fizzled out. One reason for the dollar's rebound, according to a trader, is the spike in USD/JPY--sometimes a lead indicator for USD/Asia in general--after Japanese Finance Minister Aso hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be necessary to keep the yen from strengthening too much. If USD/MYR ends Thursday above 3.1920--thereby still within the Bollinger uptrend channel--the ringgit may weaken further toward 3.2270 where the dollar will bump into the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone. USD/MYR is now 3.2030. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones]--Third-quarter GDP data from the euro zone's big two paint a rather divergent picture. French growth was estimated to have contracted by 0.1%, when a rise of exactly that amount had been expected. German growth was bang on forecasts, 0.3% on the quarter. Its stats agency says expansion was entirely driven by domestic demand (Germany has been under recent international fire for weakness here). It looks as though there is growing economic divergence in Europe's powerful core. We'll get the GDP figure for the whole bloc later this morning. (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong GDP likely grew 3.4% in the third quarter from a year earlier, slightly higher than the second quarter's 3.3% growth, according to the median forecast of five economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. On a quarter-to-quarter seasonally adjusted basis, third-quarter GDP likely expanded 0.7% from the previous quarter, compared with the second quarter's 0.8% growth, the poll shows. Economists say the expected decent GDP growth was backed by solid domestic consumption and a recovery in trade activity, with China's growth momentum picking up in the same period. However, the general decline in retail sales growth poses a downside risk to the export of services, says ANZ senior economist Raymond Yeung. "We believe the headline growth of 4Q GDP may soften a bit," he says, adding Hong Kong's property prices have shown some signs of a downward correction, hindering households' consumption appetite. The government is scheduled to release the data at 0830 GMT on Friday. (chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] The greenback is recovering from early weakness against Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - triggered by USD/JPY boosting up toward the 100.00 barrier, says one trader. The U.S. dollar, now at Y99.60 to the Japanese yen, started Thursday weak after dovish comments by Ben Bernanke and Janet Yellen reduced expectations of the Federal Reserve tapering quantitative easing. But after Japanese Finance Minister Aso hinted at the need to keep the yen from rising, USD/JPY rallied and took most USD/Asia pairs along, particularly USD/KRW, USD/SGD and USD/MYR. In Seoul, the dollar ended the day higher despite gapping sharply down at the opening bell. Against the Sing dollar, the U.S. unit has bounced over the last few hours and might even reinstate a near-term bullish technical bias on a global daily closing above 1.2460. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei rises sharply in afternoon trading, now up 2.6% at 14,943.59 as the dollar continues to rebound, now at Y99.69. The Nikkei's advance puts it in technical 'bull territory,' meaning a rise of at least 20% intraday off its recent low (14,899.02). If the index closes above 14,934.46, it will have returned to a technical 'bull market' for the first time since June. "The buying is coming from hedge funds trying to push the limits of the current weaker yen trend while going long on Nikkei futures," says Norihiro Fujito, a senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "The cash market is merely catching up." The stock market is particularly vulnerable to the moves of just a few large players, as it is currently in a low-volume season when few others are showing much interest, save for individual names, he adds. "The short-yen, long-futures strategy was what drove the Nikkei up over 80% from mid-November to late May. The fact that this is occurring again exactly a year to the day that the first Nikkei rally began [Nov. 14, 2012] is pure coincidence, albeit an interesting one; the same players have been actively pushing the yen lower for the last several days." (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 14, 2013 03:30 ET (08:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131114e9be0013v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
