\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-New Zealand/Australia Morning Call-Global markets\b0\par\par\uc2 1,064 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2013\par 19:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (UPDATE 1)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------(07:17 / 1817 GMT)----------------------\par                                                                                  \par  Stock Market\par   S&P/ASX 200  5.355.43    +36.2  NZSX 50        4,927.18   +8.52\par   DJIA        15,872.32  +50.69  Nikkei        14,876.41 +309.25\par   NASDAQ        3969.63  +3.73   FTSE           6,666.13  +36.13\par   S&P 500      1,789.93   +7.93  Hang Seng     22,463.83 +185.32\par   SPI 200 Fut  5,366.00   +1.00  TRJCRB Index     273.84   +0.35\par                                                                                  \par   Bonds (Yield)                                                   \par   AU 10 YR Bond     4.110  +0.040  US 10 YR Bond   2.711  -0.039\par   NZ 10 YR Bond     4.745  -0.005  US 30 YR Bond   3.807  -0.022\par                                                                                  \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Prev at 7pm NZST)                                   \par   AUD US$          0.9310  0.9346  NZD US$        0.8278  0.8288\par   EUR US$          1.3452  1.3465  Yen US$        100.08   99.65\par                                                                                  \par   Commodities                                                     \par   Gold (Lon)      1286.00          Silver (Lon)     20.780        \par   Gold (NY)       1278.61          Light Crude       93.76        \par   ----------------------------------------------------------------\par  Overnight market action with latest New York figures.\par     EQUITIES\par     NEW YORK - The Dow and S&P indexes climbed to fresh highs\par Thursday after U.S. Federal Reserve Chair nominee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par told a Senate committee that the Fed's accommodative policies\par would continue as long as the economy remains fragile.\par     The Dow Jones industrial average was up 42.95 points,\par or 0.27 percent, at 15,864.58. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index \par  was up 6.85 points, or 0.38 percent, at 1,788.85. The\par Nasdaq Composite Index  was down 2.27 points, or 0.06\par percent, at 3,963.30.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     LONDON - Britain's top shares rebounded on Thursday, led\par higher by energy stocks after the Federal Reserve's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par eased worries the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may start scaling back its\par stimulus programme this year. \par     The FTSE 100 closed up 36.13 points, or 0.5 percent,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 at 6,666.13. It had fallen sharply on Wednesday after the Bank of England gave a stronger forecast for the UK economy, bringing forward expectations of when interest rates will rise and sending sterling higher, hitting exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     TOKYO - The Nikkei share average jumped 2.1 percent to a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 near six-month closing high on Thursday, helped by dovish comments from Federal Reserve vice chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  and data showing the Japanese economy notched up its fourth straight quarter of growth. The benchmark Nikkei gained 309.25 points to 14,876.41, its highest close since May 22. It is up 43 percent this year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     SYDNEY - Australian shares are set to rise marginally on\par Friday tracking U.S. positive sentiment on Federal Reserve Chair\par nominee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 's accommodative policies. \par     Stock index futures rose 1 point to 5, 366, a 10.57\par point premium to the close of the underlying S&P/ASX 200 index\par .  The benchmark added 36.2 points to 5,355.4. \par     - - - -\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     NEW YORK - The dollar held gains against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday, recovering from losses the previous day, as Federal Reserve Chair nominee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  provided no negative surprises, defending the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stimulus to spur growth. The dollar rose to two-month highs against the yen to 100.14 JPY=, getting an initial headstart after Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso told a parliamentary committee that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must retain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a policy tool and be ready to take action when markets are excessively volatile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     TREASURIES \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt prices rose on Thursday as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 views from Federal Reserve Vice Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  at her Senate panel hearing on her nomination to head the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were perceived as bond friendly, stoking bids for government debt. On the open market, benchmark 10-year Treasury notes were 13/32 higher in price with a yield of 2.702 percent, down 5 basis points from late on Wednesday. Thirty-year bonds were up 27/32 after gaining more than 1 point. Their yield fell to 3.779 percent, down 5 basis points from Wednesday's close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     COMMODITIES \par     GOLD\par     NEW YORK - Gold rose on Thursday, extending its gains into a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 second session after the nominee for Federal Reserve chairman, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 , defended the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bold steps to spur growth, suggesting the massive bond-buying stimulus will continue. Spot gold was up 0.8 percent at $1,288.60 an ounce by 12:37 p.m. EST (1737 GMT), after snapping a four-day losing streak on Wednesday with a gain of nearly 1 percent. U.S. gold futures for December delivery were up $20 at $1,288.40 an ounce, with trading volume on track to finish in line with its 250-day average, preliminary Reuters data shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     BASE METALS\par     LONDON - Copper ended little changed on Thursday, near a\par three-month low, pressured by uncertainty over the timing of a\par scale-back in U.S. monetary stimulus, growing supply and poor\par economic data from Europe. \par    Benchmark copper on the London Metal Exchange closed\par at $6,992 a tonne, up marginally from $6,980 on Wednesday, after\par hitting an intra-day low of $6,940, its lowest since Aug. 7. \par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     OIL\par     NEW YORK - Brent crude oil rose more than $1 per barrel on\par Thursday, and U.S. crude pared sharp losses on signs the U.S.\par Federal Reserve will keep stimulus measures in place. \par     Brent for December delivery traded up $1.56 to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $108.68 a barrel at 1:01 p.m. EST (1801 GMT), breaking above its 200-day moving average for the first time in almost two weeks. The contract has rallied by more than 5 percent since hitting a 4-month low near $103 a barrel last week, supported by the supply outages in Libya. U.S. crude rose 33 cents to $94.21 a barrel, after settling up by 84 cents on Wednesday.\f28 \par