\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2013\par 12:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock futures were slightly lower Friday, ahead of the May nonfarm payrolls release, which has taken on added importance as officials at the Federal Reserve weigh whether to continue the asset-purchase program at the current pace.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "The markets are focused on this number, which is the most important payroll number that we've had in a long time," said \uc2 Rick Rieder\uc2 , who oversees $763 billion as BlackRock's co-head of Americas Fixed Income. "If you have a much better-than-expected number, it really introduces the question of where monetary policy is going, and that will set the trajectory for the markets."\par \par \uc2 At 1033 GMT, futures on the \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial\uc2  Average were down 0.2% at 15,010.00, S&P 500 Index futures were down 0.2% at 1,620.00 and Nasdaq 100 futures were down 0.2% at 2,945.00.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1034 GMT, the Euro Stoxx 50 was down 0.3% at 2668.97, hovering around the flat line and showing no reaction to better-than-expected German industrial production figures. Investors were likely reluctant to make any bold moves ahead of U.S. nonfarm payrolls at 1230 GMT. "The focus today is clearly centered around the U.S., as markets look more than ever for indications of how economic figures may influence the Fed's potential exit strategy," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brenda Kelly\f28 \uc2 \f2  at IG. The Stoxx 600 chemicals index was off 0.5% after a downgrade of the sector by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Among individual stocks, Vinci was up 2.4% after a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  upgrade to buy from neutral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's DAX was 0.4% lower at 8065.53, and France's CAC-40 index was down 0.2% at 3803.92.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1035 GMT, London's FTSE 100 traded down 0.2% at 6324.20, little-changed after the release of U.K. trade data showing that the deficit narrowed more than expected in April. The index was trading in an 18-point range, with investors happy to sit on their hands until the U.S. nonfarm payrolls are released at 1230 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Market anxiety may well turn into a full-blown panic attack, if nonfarm payrolls are better-than-expected this afternoon," said Interactive Investor. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BT Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 2.4% after a brokerage upgrade by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Land Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  followed close behind, up 1.5%, also after a brokerage upgrade.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the wholesale dollar selloff seen post-ECB Thursday and ahead of the looming May U.S. nonfarm payrolls report, the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs have been constricted to tight ranges in Europe, with just a touch of dollar strength seen versus the euro, sterling and Swiss franc. The yen was largely left alone after the near 400 sen slump against the dollar overnight. News flows saw the Japanese Government Pension Investment Fund announce its new asset allocation plan in late Asia, while the German Bundesbank lowered its growth forecasts for the next two years. Neither event had a noticeable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  impact. Data showed the U.K. trade gap narrowed in April as imports fell. Euro-zone peripheral bond yields were also quiet although Italian yields were just a touch lower and core government bonds were grabbing a safety bid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1037 GMT, the euro was unchanged at $1.3246, the dollar was down at Y95.96 and the pound was lower at $1.5573. September bunds were 0.63 higher at 144.08, while September gilts were up 0.63 at 116.10.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =========================== \par TOP STORIES: \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bundesbank Lowers Germany's Growth Forecast\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut its growth forecast for Europe's largest economy this year and next, tying the nation's fate to whether the euro-zone emerges from recession.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Aso Rules Out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  For Now\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said he has no intention to order direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to tackle the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent rebound, in a rare move for a Japanese official to publicly rule out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after a sharp appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU's Rehn Slams IMF over Greece\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  Economics Chief Olli Rehn had harsh words for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  Friday as he responded to a report by the IMF criticizing the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  over the Greek bailout.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy Credit Crunch Heats Up\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian bank loans to companies fell 3.7% in April from the same month a year earlier, the fastest decline seen during the country's current recession, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Italy\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =========================== \par  \par INSIGHT & ANALYSIS: \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: As Bond Prices Fall, Strategies Shift\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A sharp fall in bond prices has sent investors scurrying to protect themselves amid fears that rising interest rates will put an end to decades of strong returns, write Katy Burne, Mike Cherney and Carolyn Cui.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Turkey Leader Holds Firm on Protests\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\f28 \uc2 \f2  maintained a tough stance against antigovernment protests, pushing stocks into bear-market territory, as both supporters and detractors gathered to await his return to the country, Emre Peker and Joe Parkinson report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =========================== \par STILL TO COME \par GMT   COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 1230 US  May Employment Report \par 1230 CAN Q1  Labour productivity \par 1230 CAN May Labour Force Survey \par 1500 US      ABA economic forecast \par 1800 CAN     Weekly Financial Stats \par 1900 US  Apr Consumer Credit \par N/A  US      Obama meets Chinese \par              President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par N/A  JPN     French President \par              continues Japan visit \par N/A  GER     Deutsche Bundesbank \par              Semi-Annual Forecasts \par =========================== \par  \par OTHER NEWS: \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 S Korea Accepts Talks, But Wants Different Venue\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea accepted North Korea's proposal for working-level talks, but requested the venue be changed to Panmunjom. North Korea wanted to hold the working talks in the Kaesong joint industrial zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Soros Fund Back in Japan Market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors of all types are selling Japanese stocks, worried a recovery might not be easy for the government to engineer. But \f28 \uc2 \f2 George Soros\f28 \uc2 \f2 's firm, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soros Fund Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , is buying once again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toyota\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shows Off New Corolla Compact\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  unveiled the new sporty design for the 11th generation of its Corolla compact car--a long-delayed revamp for a model that has fallen behind its competition in styling and technology.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Charges Readied in Libor Case\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. and British authorities are preparing to bring criminal charges against former employees of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  for their alleged roles trying to manipulate benchmark interest rates, according to people familiar with the plans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's GDP Grows 0.8%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean economy improved in the first quarter from the prior three months, but at a slightly slower pace than previously forecast.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glaxo's Avandia Wins FDA Victory\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A government panel called for easing restrictions on the onetime big-selling diabetes drug Avandia, in a remarkable about-face nearly three years after concerns over heart risk led regulators to curtail use of the drug.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jonathan Robins at jonathan.robins@dowjones.com [ 07-06-13 1056GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70737   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130607e967000gm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
