\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Easing Opens Room for More Polish Rate Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2013\par 13:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW--The wave of cheap cash from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's newly boosted easing measures has indirectly repaired the damage inflicted on Polish markets by the recent turmoil in Cyprus.\par \par \uc2 Bond prices across Europe have jumped on the expectation of a rush of investment from Japan as private investors there search for higher yields elsewhere. Wednesday, the head of \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Marek Belka, identified this as the reason for a rally in the zloty and in Polish government bonds.\par \par \uc2 "Unprecedented monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused a rally on any sovereign bonds that have yields over zero. As a result Polish bond yields dropped to all-time lows and obviously the zloty strengthened," the Mr. Belka said in his monthly press conference.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts think this may help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease monetary conditions.\par \par \uc2 Since last week, Polish debt has clearly strengthened, with five-year bond yields dropping below 3% for a first time ever, and the zloty pushing higher. The euro has fallen from PLN4.20 against the zloty last Thursday to the lowest levels since January, at around PLN4.09. Similar trends have been seen elsewhere in European emerging markets, with Turkish debt and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also climbing.\par \par \uc2 The climb in the zloty will depress Polish import prices--a factor that analysts say is likely to weaken Polish inflation, which has already dropped sharply over last six months to hit 1.3% in February, below the bank's tolerance range. This may open the door to further Polish easing, which was previously thought to have been halted after a half-percentage-point cut in key rates in March.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, inflows from Japanese investors are likely to alleviate some concerns among Polish rate setters that the increase in foreign investment in the country's bond market could heighten the risk of a sharp sell-off should those inflows be reversed, opening the door to even more easing, BRE Bank economist Ernest Pytlarczyk said.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, I think there is a significant room for interest rate cuts, at least 0.5 percentage point," Mr. Pytlarczyk said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Belka Wednesday downplayed some of his earlier concerns, saying a higher-than-expected proportion of foreign investors seem to be committed to Polish debt over the longer term.\par \par \uc2 The 10-strong monetary rate panel has consistently bucked the global monetary easing trend, keeping interest rates above inflation at a time when many regional peers and global central banks have slashed borrowing costs to close to zero.\par \par \uc2 But a rapid slowdown last year in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's largest emerging-market economy, an uncertain outlook for 2013 and rapidly decelerating inflation has prompted the panel to cut rates by a total of 1.5 percentage point between November 2012 and March 2013.\par \par \uc2 Poland's growth slowed to 2% in 2012, and is expected to ease further to about 1.3%-1.5% in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Write to Patryk Wasilewski at patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com [ 11-04-13 1137GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130411e94b000m2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
