\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Central Banks May Not Need High Reserves, Fed Paper Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Walter  \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2013\par 04:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Since 2008, central banks from China to Japan to Switzerland have squirreled away money and engineered an unprecedented expansion in their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings, typically with the goal of trying to manage their exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 But a new paper from the researchers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  argues that for many of those institutions, the pool of money might be getting so unwieldy that the benefits of this supposed "insurance policy" are outweighed by the costs.\par \par \uc2 China's gargantuan $3 trillion store of reserves attracts the most attention, but the paper also singles out central banks in industrialized countries, including Japan and Switzerland, for their aggressive reserve accumulation.\par \par \uc2 With both the financial crisis of 2008 and the subsequent euro crisis boosting "safe haven" inflows into both the Japanese yen and Swiss franc, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  have periodically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to buy up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and temper that appreciation trend, all in a bid to protect their exporters' competitiveness. But managing the massive portfolios of reserves that they have built up carries risks, the authors of the report, Linda Goldberg, Cindy Hull and Sarah Stein say.\par \par \uc2 What's more, increasingly there are other tools available to central banks seeking to manage their exchange rates than merely buying and selling reserves, they argue.\par \par \uc2 "While the scale of international financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions has grown tremendously over time, it does not necessarily follow that the reserve balances of industrialized countries should expand correspondingly," according to the Fed paper, which was posted Monday on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's website.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland may be the poster child for rapid reserve growth. The SNB's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets have jumped 158% since the beginning of January 2010 and equaled $532.8 billion as of February. That is about 80% of the country's annual gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's paper identifies two major risks for large reserve accumulators. The first is the so-called opportunity cost: most central banks are only allowed to invest in relatively low-yielding assets, providing minimal returns. Switzerland stands out here as an exception, as its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can invest in a wider range of assets, including corporate bonds and equities.\par \par \uc2 The other is the possibility that exchange rates will eventually shift substantially, potentially reducing the value of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large holdings. The SNB, for example, has bought large amounts of euros, leaving it exposed to the risk that the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may weaken.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  points out that successful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  historically haven't always required large amounts of reserves. Central banks are often most effective when they act in a coordinated way with other central banks, or when they use the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to signal the direction they intend to take in the more traditional aspects of monetary policy via interest rates and buying and selling home-country assets.\par \par \uc2 Banks that build up reserves as insurance against a sudden stop in liquidity, such as occurred during the Asian financial crisis in the 1990s, might do well to consider alternatives. For example, the Fed's swap agreements that provided dollar funding to foreign countries at the height of the financial crisis allowed those central banks to avoid using their reserves.\par \par \uc2 At the very least, the Fed paper argues the topic needs more discussion.\par \par \uc2 "Considerable uncertainty exists about the level of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves needed by industrialized countries," the report concludes.\par \par \uc2 Write to Matthew Walter at matthew.walter@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 02-04-13 0228GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130402e9420003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
