\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Nominee Kuroda Pledges Deflation Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 03:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO--The government's nominee for next head of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  pledged Monday to undertake every option to overcome years of price declines, but he appeared less confident about adopting the controversial idea of lowering the short-term interest rate floor, already near zero.\par \par \uc2 In another round of a confirmation hearings before parliament, \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , president of the Asian Development Bank, reaffirmed his commitment to do whatever it takes to achieve a landmark 2.0% inflation target in about two years if approved as BOJ governor.\par \par \uc2 He added that since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures so far have been "insufficient" to get Japan out of deflation for 15 years, "further easing action is necessary."\par \par \uc2 Financial markets already take for granted that the BOJ will take additional easing action at its policy-setting meeting in April when Mr. Kuroda will probably make his debut. The likely options include extending the maturities of Japanese government bonds the BOJ buys from the current three years, and bringing forward the start of open-ended asset purchasing method planned next year, as well as ramping up purchases of risk assets.\par \par \uc2 But the market's focus has shifted to whether a change in leadership will open the way for fresh and unprecedented easing steps that current Gov. \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  was loath to adopt, such as lowering or eliminating the 0.1% interest paid on excess reserves that commercial banks deposit with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the upper house confirmation hearing committee, Mr. Kuroda said: "There are pros and cons on this. I would like to discuss this at policy board (if approved as governor)."\par \par \uc2 Explaining that the option is also under discussion in Europe and the U.S, Mr. Kuroda said maintaining the interest rate could aid smooth functioning of the short-term money market.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Mr. Kuroda said there is a view that the interest-rate floor has become an obstacle to further easing, and thus there is even debate that the rate should be negative.\par \par \uc2 Policy board member \uc2 Koji Ishida\uc2  proposed in December scrapping the interest rate on the excess reserves, but the proposal was voted down eight to one. Mr. Ishida, a former executive at Sumitomo Mitsui Bank, argued that the measure could help reduce the attractiveness of the yen as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another board member, \uc2 Sayuri Shirai\uc2 , also said in an interview in January that she is open to the option, but careful discussion is needed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda also said "there is no need to consider buying foreign bonds' as another option for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing. BOJ foreign bond purchases by the BOJ, which may be regarded as a kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," risks raising concerns from overseas that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift exports. Mr. Kuroda, a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, said the finance ministry is responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the option of guiding interest rates lower by using interest rate swaps, Mr. Kuroda said there are various debates on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should step into the swap and other derivatives markets is a good thing or not, and the matter requires "sufficient" debate.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tatsuo Ito at tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 03-10-13 2205ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130311e93b0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
