\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five facts about ADB chief nominee Nakao\b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 7 (Reuters) - Japan will nominate its top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, Takehiko Nakao, to become head of the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2 , succeeding incumbent \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kuroda has been tapped as Japan's next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor.\par \par \uc2 The Manila-based ADB will hold an election to choose a new president in time for its annual meeting in May.\par \par \uc2 Following are five facts about Nakao:\par \par \uc2 - The 57-year-old vice finance minister for international affairs has represented Japan at \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  meetings of economic powers since his appointment a year-and-a-half ago.\par \par \uc2 - A career bureaucrat at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nakao has a wealth of contacts among global policymakers. He has strived to enhance financial cooperation between Japan and its Asian neighbours, including China and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 - Nakao oversaw \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in 2011 to prevent the yen's rise from derailing the export-reliant economy's recovery from the devastating March 2011 earthquake and tsunami. Japan's solo actions drew criticism from the United States.\par \par \uc2 - A graduate of the \uc2 University of Tokyo\uc2 's Faculty of Economics, Nakao obtained an MBA from the University of California at Berkeley in 1982. He also worked for the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  from 1994 to 1997, and served as financial official at the Japanese embassy in Washington from 2005 to 2007, becoming well acquainted with U.S. affairs.\par \par \uc2 - In a departure from the stereotypical image of Japanese elite bureaucrats, Nakao stresses the need to strike a balance between the time he spends at home and at the ministry. He likes to cook meals for his family at weekends.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ADB-NAKAO (FACTBOX) | ABX | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130310e93700009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
