\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG: Reflationist Diehards Get Their Second Choice\b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2013\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Japan Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/japanrealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/japanrealtime }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 Markets have been pleased by Japanese prime minister Shinzo Abe's choice for the country's next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, with the benchmark stock index rising 2% and the yen tumbling (a good thing), after reports that former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  czar Haruhiko Kuroda had snagged the spot. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Kuroda Faces 40% Pay Cut at BOJ \par  \par    -- Investors Brace for the Choice of Japan's New \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \par  \par    -- Loose Lips Sink BOJ Governorships \par  \par    -- Video: Abenomics Wins Over A Bank of Japan Skeptic \par  \par    -- China Scrambles Monetary Jets Over BOJ Airspace \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Kuroda is an ex-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official with a track record of criticizing the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's performance, whose experience directing a period of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the early 2000s means he's used to being on the hot seat, internationally. Assuming he's approved by parliament, he's expected to push for considerably more aggressive monetary easing from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after he takes over on March 19. In recent interviews, he's said he thinks pumping a lot more money into the economy could jumpstart inflation in the next few years -- a view many in Japan have dubbed "reflationist."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, reflationist diehards took to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  shortly after the news started to trickle out Sunday night to protest that Mr. Abe's choice wasn't radical enough, since most BOJ governors of the past few decades have first had careers either in the BOJ or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Some even suspected that the early media reports were leaks by MOF mean to cement Mr. Kuroda's chances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It goes to show how desperate the MOF is," said one such tweet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The choice of the reflationist Twitterati: Kikuo Iwata, an economist at Gakushuin University who's been long known for battling with the BOJ over the effectiveness of monetary policy to defeat deflation and seen as the champion of reflationist thinking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "PM Abe, please make Kikuo Iwata the next BOJ governor. That would make clear the 'regime change," said one tweet, referring to the phrase used by Mr. Abe to describe his idea of aggressive monetary easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's important that Japan is serious (about beating deflation). I think Kikuo Iwata can make a strong case for that," tweeted another.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the diehards are unlikely to be getting a Gov. Iwata, their wishes weren't neglected by Mr. Abe, who himself has been heavily influenced by the reflationist school of thought. Monday morning, Mr. Iwata said that Mr. Abe had asked him to take one of two deputy governor posts that's being filled at the same time. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 25, 2013 20:38 ET (01:38 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130226e92q000e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
