\par\par \b \uc2 NEWSMAKER-Kuroda, fan of detective novels, looks to solve BOJ mystery\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stanley White and Linda Sieg   \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2013\par 00:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 25 (Reuters) - An avid reader of detective novels, \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  may now get a chance to try to unravel the mystery of engineering Japanese economic growth if the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  chief is confirmed as the next Bank of Japan governor.\par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Shinzo Abe is expected to nominate the 68-year-old former finance ministry official for the BOJ's top post, sources told Reuters on Monday. Abe is trying to transform the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a bold deflation fighter to revive Japan's stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2  A fluent English speaker with a masters degree from \uc2 Oxford University\uc2 , Kuroda served as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat after the Asian financial crisis in the late 1990s and as ADB president since 2005, putting him in regular contact with global policymakers.\par \par \uc2  Kuroda will likely need to harness his international network and experience as BOJ governor to counter criticism from other countries that Japan's aggressive monetary easing is intended to weaken the yen and give its exporters an unfair advantage.\par \par \uc2  "Kuroda can hold the stage with anybody - Bernanke, the Bundesbank or the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 ," said Jesper Koll, head of equities research at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in Tokyo, who has known Kuroda for two decades.\par \par \uc2  Kuroda may also be more keen to communicate BOJ policy than incumbent \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2 , who was regularly criticised as being too coy about playing up the effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2  When guiding \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat -- a post he held from 1999 to 2003 -- Kuroda was keen to send out the message, through the media, that Tokyo was indeed stepping into the market and serious about stemming yen rises.\par \par \uc2  People who have worked with him describe him as soft-spoken and approachable, but a demanding boss who would ask a lot from subordinates. "He is very energetic and loves work," said an ADB official who works under him.\par \par \uc2  AGGRESSIVE ENOUGH\par \par \uc2  The flip side of Kuroda's global resume may be a lack of extensive contacts among domestic business leaders and bankers, a gap that could, however, be made up for by his deputies.\par \par \uc2  Abe will need to get a signoff on Kuroda from opposition parties since his Liberal Democratic Party and its junior coalition partner control parliament's lower house but lack a majority in the upper chamber, which must approve the nominee.\par \par \uc2  But Kuroda might fit the bill as a candidate radical enough to fit Abe's criteria but moderate enough for the biggest opposition Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ), where some heavyweights have taken issue with Abe's aggressive stance.\par \par \uc2  Some Japanese politicians and policymakers worry that if Kuroda leaves his post at the ADB before it expires, Japan would risk losing the coveted job to another country such as China.\par \par \uc2  Traditionally, a Japanese finance official is chosen to head the Manila-based ADB, much as a European by tradition runs the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and an American the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  That might be the price to pay for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor who fully supports Abe's push for aggressive easing and can articulate Japan's policies to an international audience.\par \par \uc2  Kuroda has said the BOJ's 2 percent inflation target, set in January, can be met in two years, contrary to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's argument that it has not set a strict timeframe for achieving that level. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda would succeed Shirakawa, 63, a career central banker who was made governor in 2008 as a compromise choice and is due to leave with his two deputies on March 19.\par \par \uc2  Under Shirakawa's leadership, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut interest rates almost to zero and kept injecting cash into the economy, but has been criticised for being too cautious. \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-BOJ/KURODA (NEWSMAKER) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | J | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130224e92o000dp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
