\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  GLOBAL CALL: Our Take on the Day's Big  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  News\b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2013\par 13:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Global Call covers the main talking points from a news meeting involving DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader editors in New York, London and Singapore, as well as other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hot spots when warranted.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Brian Baskin \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1. The yen is slightly stronger as the first hints of dissent over monetary policy within Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe's administration may be forming. Finance Minister Taro Aso said his government had "no intention" of asking the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to buy foreign bonds to weaken the yen and isn't considering revisions to the law governing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "at this moment." Those remarks came after Mr. Abe said changing the law was a possibility if the BOJ can't meet its 2% inflation target. Foreign bond buying is a touchy subject for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it could provoke the sort of criticism about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it managed to avoid at the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  meeting over the weekend. However, Mr. Abe is fighting worries his campaign push to end deflation could sputter. Minutes from the BOJ's January meeting show the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considering buying long-term Japanese bonds, a weaker easing alternative if foreign bond buying is off the table. The yen was recently at 93.50 to the dollar, from 93.85 before Mr. Aso spoke.\par \par \uc2 2. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  indicated via its February meeting minutes that it's not interested in cutting interest rates, despite the stream of mixed to poor economic data. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sees the effects of its previous cuts--1.75 percentage points since November 2011--working their way into the economy. The Australian dollar rose slightly after the minutes came out, trading recently at $1.0346.\par \par \uc2 3. Germany's ZEW indicator of investor economic expectations shot to a nearly three-year high of 48.2 in February from 31.5 in January. While a sign that the market sees euro-zone conditions improving, the euro itself barely budged afterward. Traders see the ZEW as a curtain-raiser for the \uc2 Ifo Institute\uc2 's business confidence survey and PMIs coming at the end of the week. The impending Italian elections are also likely to put a damper on market activity.\par \par \uc2 4. The Swedish krona is up after unemployment rose less than expected. Last week, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  indicated it would hold off on interest rate cuts; now it has fresh data to back up its hawkish stance. The path of least resistance is up for the krona, despite it being at a nearly 20-year high, Citi says.\par \par \uc2 (Brian Baskin is assistant managing editor for the Americas at DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  news service jointly produced by Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.) [ 19-02-13 1203GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020130219e92j0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
