\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Seoul Is a  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Pacifist\b0\par\par\uc2 By Duncan Mavin   \par \uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2013\par 01:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 If Japan's aggressive talk on monetary policy threatens to spark a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, then its neighbor, South Korea, is wisely refusing to engage by tinkering with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing exporters from the two countries like \uc2 Samsung\uc2  and \uc2 Sony\uc2 , \uc2 Hyundai\uc2  and Toyota, often go head-to-head.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rapid weakening should make Japan's exporters more competitive. The won is up more than 6% against the yen since the start of 2013 and gained about 20% last year.\par \par \uc2 Fight fans might have expected a strong response from Seoul when \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  met Thursday. Instead, \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its benchmark interest rate untouched at 2.75%.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's inaction makes sense, especially as the impact of the weaker yen may be partly offset by other factors. For instance, South Korean exporters may be among the biggest beneficiaries of Japan's political spat with China that is denting demand for Japanese autos and other products in Asia's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Also, the weaker yen means energy costs for Japanese manufacturers are rising.\par \par \uc2 Since the Fukushima natural disaster that led to the closure of most of Japan's nuclear facilities, the country has become increasingly energy-dependent on shipments of liquefied natural gas from overseas.\par \par \uc2 What's more, favorable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements will only partially help Japanese companies with overseas sales. Until a Japanese consumer-electronics maker can stock shelves with a killer smartphone, yen weakening probably won't do much damage to sales of \uc2 Samsung\uc2 's Galaxy.\par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop--and with signs of an uptick in external demand from the U.S. and China, in particular--cutting rates in South Korea could needlessly risk fueling inflation or fueling household debt.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  will surely monitor the yen's slide carefully, though a rate cut is unlikely to curb won strengthening anyway. A quarter-percentage point cut brings rates to 2.5%, still higher than the very low interest rates in the U.S., the euro zone and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is an option, though Seoul's capacity to do so effectively is very limited compared with Tokyo's greater firepower given the relative size of the South Korean and Japanese economies.\par \par \uc2 Despite the yen's recent decline, the won is still about 3% weaker against the yen than its average over the past ten years.\par \par \uc2 A deeper fall in the value of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a prolonged period would undoubtedly put pressure on South Korean exports. For now, though, it's safest for Seoul to remain on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Write to Duncan Mavin at duncan.mavin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (Duncan Mavin is Asia Editor for Heard on the Street. He can be reached at +852-2832-2397, or at duncan.mavin@wsj.com.)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120130215e92f0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
