\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Japan Braces For Uncomfortable Spotlight On Yen at G-20\b0\par\par\uc2 1,014 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2013\par 13:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Japan braces for attacks on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at upcoming G-20 meeting in Moscow\par \par \uc2 --Finance Minister Taro Aso, other Japanese officials, describe recent \uc2 G-7\uc2  statement as defense of Japan's policies\par \par \uc2 --Japan's struggles to deal with the yen once again the focus of global debates\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi\par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Japan once again heads into a summit of global economic policymakers braced for attacks on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The uncomfortable spotlight looming in Moscow this Friday follows days of delicate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomacy for Japanese officials, who seemed reluctantly prodded by allies into signing a seven-nation statement against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation inspired by the yen's recent sharp drop, who then portrayed the declaration as an endorsement of Japan's actions.\par \par \uc2 "Other countries called for it ... it wasn't like we encouraged it," Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso told reporters Tuesday following the release of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  statement affirming that economic stimulus polices are aimed at lifting domestic growth and that "we will not target exchange rates."\par \par \uc2 "My sense is that we just had a general debate on this matter," Mr. Aso said. "The talks just became livelier."\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Aso and other Japanese officials described the statement as a defense of Japan's actions, particularly its recently aggressive monetary policy, which has weakened the yen. Mr. Aso was asked if the statement was intended to discourage finger pointing over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the meeting in Russia of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  , a larger group that includes countries like South Korea and China, which have been openly critical of Japan's economic policies. "That's right," Mr. Aso replied.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets seesawed in the hours after the statement was released, with the yen weakening, then strengthening, as analysts and traders debated whether the statement was intended as an endorsement or a rebuke of Japan.\par \par \uc2 The confusion was fueled in part by the fact that the \uc2 G-7\uc2  countries haven't been unified in their comments about Japan in recent days, with Germany and France criticizing Japan, and the U.S. seeming to offer some backing. In general, \uc2 G-7\uc2  leaders have been critical of comments by Japanese officials that have suggested they were trying to weaken the yen, but have supported Japan's monetary policy which, after all, mirrors actions taken in recent years by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "There has been some concerns that associated with those major, very positive developments in macro policy (in Japan), that Japanese authorities were targeting a certain level of the exchange rate," Bank of Canada Gov. Mark Carney said this week. "There have been discussions at the \uc2 G-7\uc2  about this, I'm sure there'll be discussions this weekend at the G-20."\par \par \uc2 What is clear is that Japan's struggles to deal with the yen are again the focus of global economic policy debates. In the 1980s and early 1990s, the other \uc2 G-7\uc2  countries regularly pushed Japan to strengthen the yen and curb its trade surplus. Through the mid-1990s, the fear was Japan's financial collapse would drag down the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 Japan last found itself in a similarly uncomfortable spot in 2006-2007. The euro's sustained rise against the yen around that time drew heated complaints from European officials, including then French Trade Minister \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  , now head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 At a meeting of \uc2 G-7\uc2  finance heads in Singapore in autumn 2006, Japan bowed to European pressure and publicly acknowledged that the yen's drop versus the euro had been "a little volatile." In Germany the following year, \uc2 G-7\uc2  finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors attacked yen weakness fueled by Japan's low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, Japanese officials have also gone to great pains to portray \uc2 G-7\uc2  declarations as endorsements--tacit or explicit--of their attempts to steer the yen.\par \par \uc2 When foreign nations make no verbal objection to Tokyo's policy during international conferences, Japanese officials often put special emphasis on that, trying to signal to markets that "no objection" means approval.\par \par \uc2 The G-20 leaders summit in November 2011 in Cannes, France took place a few months after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to try to cap the yen's rise, and when it was continuing with unannounced "stealth" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During this meeting, then-Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda explained the reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And "there were no comments" from other nations, he later said. He refused to elaborate, leaving many traders with the impression \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had U.S. backing. The next month, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  issued a harsh reprimand against \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials also declared victory with a tweak in a statement from a meeting of G-20 finance chiefs in Gyeongju, South Korea in October 2010.\par \par \uc2 Instead of just repeating the mantra that any excessive exchange-rate movement would be bad for economies, the G-20 statement added that advanced economies "will be vigilant" against them. No G-20 officials emphasized any significance in the change, except Japanese policymakers led by Mr. Noda, who was finance minister at the time.\par \par \uc2 "Until now, we haven't gone beyond acknowledging that excessive volatility or disorderly moves in exchange rates have a negative impact," Mr. Noda said. "Yet, 'vigilant' has a more active meaning, like we will monitor market movements and cooperate as appropriate. We can also interpret it to mean that the relationship between the dollar, the yen and the euro will become more stable."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda's remarks left observers with the impression he was trying to convince the market that the U.S. and Europe were now more willing to approve \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there were excessive rises in the yen. That seemed to do the trick, as the yen stabilized for a few months.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 13, 2013 07:59 ET (12:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130213e92d004he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
